{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94RQTM1JF4RLN2WZZ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LLVYeZr6HpkAY3mq3cSwqn","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1389,"cache_read_input_tokens":31410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1389},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"f3eeb371-61ba-4414-9046-1c3cb713c62e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:33.137Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94R9px9QSWHiu5Ucd1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LLVYeZr6HpkAY3mq3cSwqn":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LLVYeZr6HpkAY3mq3cSwqn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Pixel Engine 0.46.0\n2\t\n3\tA WebGPU engine that renders 3D scenes as authentic pixel art (WebGPU only), with\n4\tRapier physics, a platformer character with a full animation set, pixel HUD, audio, particles\n5\tand a look system (filters per layer). This folder is everything a game needs.\n6\t\n7\tRead in this order:\n8\t\n9\t1. `GUIDE.md` (~15k tokens): how to host a game, the mental model, a complete game, recipes, the rules and common mistakes. Read it whole.\n10\t2. `API.md` (~62k tokens): every export with its signature and doc. Search it; don't read it whole.\n11\t3. `game.js`: the guide's quick start, a complete game. Edit it into yours.\n12\t4. `ASSETS.md` (~4k tokens), when the game builds with models and textures: the library, the format, the loop and the style rules. Read it whole; `LIBRARY.md` (~44k tokens) lists every model and texture, built in or from a pack: search it (or filter `library.json`).\n13\t\n14\t| File | What |\n15\t| --- | --- |\n16\t| `pixel-engine.js` | The engine, 4750 KB: one ES module with three.js (`THREE`, `TSL`), Rapier and its wasm, the hero kit, the built-in models (`assets/coin.glb`, `assets/hero.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine CSS. Never edit it. |\n17\t| `index.html`, `game.js` | A game to start from: serve this folder over HTTP and open `index.html` |\n18\t| `examples/` | The guides' other recipes as pages (`side-scroller`, `top-down`, `looks`, `market`) and the modules they import (`market-models.js`) |\n19\t| `check.mjs` | `node check.mjs [page] [--keys KeyD*60,Space*4] [--cameras] [--looks a,b]`: plays the page in a real browser, saves screenshots, fails on any error. Run it before you hand a game back. `node check.mjs --headless game.js` plays the game in Node with no browser and replays it (GUIDE.md section 8); `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws your models and textures (ASSETS.md) |\n20\t| `ASSETS.md`, `LIBRARY.md`, `CREDITS.md` | Models and textures as data; the built-in library and the imported packs, by family; where everything the kit carries came from (ship it with your game) |\n21\t| `library.json` | Every model and texture as data (id, kind, family, pack, tags, size, licence): filter it to find what a game needs |\n22\t| `packs/` | Imported packs (`packs/index.json` lists them): `loadPack('packs/<id>.json')` |\n23\t| `pixel-engine.d.ts`, `types/` | TypeScript declarations (optional) |\n24\t| `CHANGELOG.md` | What changed between engine versions |\n25\t| `LICENSE` | The engine's licence (MIT) |\n26\t| `manifest.json` | Version, build, where newer versions are (`latest`, `releases`, `source`) and every file with its size |\n27\t\n28\tServe it: `npx serve .` or `python3 -m http.server` here, then open http://localhost:3000/ (or :8000).\n29\tES modules don't load from `file://`.\n30\t\n31\tNewer versions: the newest release of the kit is always https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, and every release stays\n32\tat https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases with what changed in it. To build one from\n33\tthe engine's repository instead: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight && cd riftlight && npm ci && npm run bundle`\n34\tmakes `bundle/pixel-engine/`. To upgrade a game, see GUIDE.md, section 10.\n35\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"8a2602ba-ab68-47e7-b8ea-c847e2e0d72d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:33.147Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/README.md","content":"# Pixel Engine 0.46.0\n\nA WebGPU engine that renders 3D scenes as authentic pixel art (WebGPU only), with\nRapier physics, a platformer character with a full animation set, pixel HUD, audio, particles\nand a look system (filters per layer). This folder is everything a game needs.\n\nRead in this order:\n\n1. `GUIDE.md` (~15k tokens): how to host a game, the mental model, a complete game, recipes, the rules and common mistakes. Read it whole.\n2. `API.md` (~62k tokens): every export with its signature and doc. Search it; don't read it whole.\n3. `game.js`: the guide's quick start, a complete game. Edit it into yours.\n4. `ASSETS.md` (~4k tokens), when the game builds with models and textures: the library, the format, the loop and the style rules. Read it whole; `LIBRARY.md` (~44k tokens) lists every model and texture, built in or from a pack: search it (or filter `library.json`).\n\n| File | What |\n| --- | --- |\n| `pixel-engine.js` | The engine, 4750 KB: one ES module with three.js (`THREE`, `TSL`), Rapier and its wasm, the hero kit, the built-in models (`assets/coin.glb`, `assets/hero.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine CSS. Never edit it. |\n| `index.html`, `game.js` | A game to start from: serve this folder over HTTP and open `index.html` |\n| `examples/` | The guides' other recipes as pages (`side-scroller`, `top-down`, `looks`, `market`) and the modules they import (`market-models.js`) |\n| `check.mjs` | `node check.mjs [page] [--keys KeyD*60,Space*4] [--cameras] [--looks a,b]`: plays the page in a real browser, saves screenshots, fails on any error. Run it before you hand a game back. `node check.mjs --headless game.js` plays the game in Node with no browser and replays it (GUIDE.md section 8); `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws your models and textures (ASSETS.md) |\n| `ASSETS.md`, `LIBRARY.md`, `CREDITS.md` | Models and textures as data; the built-in library and the imported packs, by family; where everything the kit carries came from (ship it with your game) |\n| `library.json` | Every model and texture as data (id, kind, family, pack, tags, size, licence): filter it to find what a game needs |\n| `packs/` | Imported packs (`packs/index.json` lists them): `loadPack('packs/<id>.json')` |\n| `pixel-engine.d.ts`, `types/` | TypeScript declarations (optional) |\n| `CHANGELOG.md` | What changed between engine versions |\n| `LICENSE` | The engine's licence (MIT) |\n| `manifest.json` | Version, build, where newer versions are (`latest`, `releases`, `source`) and every file with its size |\n\nServe it: `npx serve .` or `python3 -m http.server` here, then open http://localhost:3000/ (or :8000).\nES modules don't load from `file://`.\n\nNewer versions: the newest release of the kit is always https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, and every release stays\nat https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases with what changed in it. To build one from\nthe engine's repository instead: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight && cd riftlight && npm ci && npm run bundle`\nmakes `bundle/pixel-engine/`. To upgrade a game, see GUIDE.md, section 10.\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LLVYeZr6HpkAY3mq3cSwqn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94RQTM1JF4RLN2WZZ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0158NkGbiS4Vg22n635n7VwK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/GUIDE.md","allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1389,"cache_read_input_tokens":31410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1389},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"474a5da2-96d3-49d4-b377-66eb68f4cde6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:33.961Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94R9px9QSWHiu5Ucd1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0158NkGbiS4Vg22n635n7VwK":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/GUIDE.md","allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0158NkGbiS4Vg22n635n7VwK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Pixel Engine: the guide\n2\t\n3\t> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\n4\t\n5\tThis guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\n6\t`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\n7\twritten to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\n8\tneed a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\n9\t\n10\tContents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\n11\t6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\n12\t\n13\t## 1. Host it\n14\t\n15\tA game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\tmy-game/\n19\t  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\n20\t  index.html          a page with <div id=\"app\"> that loads game.js as a module\n21\t  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\n22\t  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tThe kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\n26\tSplit a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\n27\t\n28\t- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\n29\t  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\n30\t- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\n31\t  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\n32\t  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\n33\t  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\n34\t- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\n35\t  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\n36\t  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\n37\t- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\n38\t  browser without it is told so (\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\"). Phones and tablets\n39\t  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\n40\t- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\n41\t  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\n42\t  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\n43\t  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\n44\t\n45\t## 2. Mental model\n46\t\n47\t**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\n48\tshapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\n49\tresolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\n50\tup by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\n51\tpipeline. A **look** can change that: other palettes, CRT, cel shading, or clean\n52\tfull-resolution rendering, per layer (section 4, *Looks*).\n53\t\n54\t**The engine owns the machinery; a game is a plain object with hooks.** The engine owns the\n55\trenderer, camera rig, physics stepping, the sun and ambient light, input, audio, particles,\n56\tthe pixel HUD, the loading screen and the debug tools. You write a class:\n57\t\n58\t| Hook | When | Do here |\n59\t| --- | --- | --- |\n60\t| `name`, `assets` | before start | the title; models to start downloading early |\n61\t| `async setup(ctx)` | once | build the level: meshes, colliders, models, characters, triggers |\n62\t| `fixedUpdate(ctx, dt)` | 60 times a second, before each physics step | movement, forces, anything gameplay that must be exact |\n63\t| `update(ctx, dt)` | every rendered frame, after physics | animation, visuals, pickups, HUD |\n64\t| `cameraTarget(ctx)` | every frame | return the `Vector3` the camera follows |\n65\t| `status(ctx)` | tooling | a one-line summary (`'coins 3/4'`), shown by the check tool |\n66\t| `fingerprint(ctx)` | tooling | the game's own state as a string (positions, counters), part of `engine.fingerprint()` so a replay can be compared |\n67\t| `snapshot(ctx)`, `restore(ctx, state)`, `beforeRestore(ctx)` | save-states | the game's own state as plain data (as it is, not through JSON), and back (`await engine.snapshot()`, `await engine.restore(snap)`); physics bodies are the engine's: keep their handles; `beforeRestore` leaves a mode with its own camera first |\n68\t| `onCameraChange(ctx)`, `eyePosition(ctx)`, `dispose(ctx)` | optional | camera swaps, first-person eye, cleanup |\n69\t| `ready(ctx)` | optional: when the engine starts running the game, and after each `loadGame` | page handles for tools (`window.__MYGAME__`) |\n70\t\n71\t`ctx` (the `GameContext`) is the same object in every hook: `ctx.scene` (three.js scene),\n72\t`ctx.physics`, `ctx.input`, `ctx.camera`, `ctx.audio`, `ctx.particles`, `ctx.hud`,\n73\t`ctx.lights`, `ctx.palette`, `ctx.loadModel`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.random` and `ctx.engine`.\n74\t\n75\t**Physics owns positions; visuals follow.** Bodies move in fixed 60 Hz steps (Rapier).\n76\tMeshes are interpolated between steps, so motion is smooth at any frame rate. A character\n77\tcontroller moves its body in `fixedUpdate`; its model is posed in `update`.\n78\t\n79\t**Scale.** One unit is one metre, Y is up, the ground is usually at y = 0. The hero is 1.6 m\n80\ttall, runs at 6.5 m/s and jumps 1.7 m (double jump 2.4 m, triple 3.5 m; it also grabs ledges).\n81\tAt the default zoom the camera shows about 13.5 m vertically, so one art pixel is about\n82\t5 cm: give shapes at least 0.25 m so they read.\n83\t\n84\t## 3. Quick start\n85\t\n86\tA complete game, and the kit's `game.js`. Walk with WASD or the arrows, jump with Space,\n87\tcollect the four coins.\n88\t\n89\t```js quickstart\n90\t// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\n91\timport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n92\t\n93\tconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n94\t\n95\t/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\n96\tfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n97\t  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n98\t  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n99\t  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n100\t  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n101\t  return mesh;\n102\t}\n103\t\n104\tclass CoinGarden {\n105\t  name = 'Coin Garden';\n106\t  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\n107\t  coins = [];\n108\t  collected = 0;\n109\t  won = false;\n110\t  target = new Vector3();\n111\t  ground = new Vector3();\n112\t\n113\t  async setup(ctx) {\n114\t    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n115\t    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\n116\t    scene.add(\n117\t      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\n118\t        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\n119\t        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\n120\t        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\n121\t        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\n122\t      ]),\n123\t    );\n124\t    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\n125\t    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\n126\t    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\n127\t      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\n128\t      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n129\t      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\n130\t      scene.add(mesh);\n131\t      const item = { mesh, taken: false };\n132\t      physics.trigger({ cylinder: { halfHeight: 0.6, radius: 0.5 } }, [x, y, z], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.collect(ctx, item) });\n133\t      this.coins.push(item);\n134\t    }\n135\t    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\n136\t    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n137\t    this.model = hero.scene;\n138\t    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n139\t    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n140\t    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n141\t    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 4], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n142\t    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n143\t  }\n144\t\n145\t  collect(ctx, item) {\n146\t    item.taken = true;\n147\t    item.mesh.visible = false;\n148\t    this.collected++;\n149\t    ctx.audio.play('coin');\n150\t    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', item.mesh.position);\n151\t  }\n152\t\n153\t  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n154\t    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input); // filled in place after the first step\n155\t    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input); // movement and forces: fixed 60 Hz step\n156\t  }\n157\t\n158\t  update(ctx, dt) {\n159\t    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha); // pose and interpolate\n160\t    const feet = this.model.position;\n161\t    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.ground.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n162\t    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n163\t    for (const c of this.coins) if (!c.taken) c.mesh.rotation.y += dt * 3;\n164\t    if (!this.won && this.collected === this.coins.length) {\n165\t      this.won = true;\n166\t      this.hero.celebrate();\n167\t      ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n168\t    }\n169\t    ctx.hud.clear();\n170\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n171\t    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n172\t  }\n173\t\n174\t  cameraTarget() {\n175\t    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 4);\n176\t    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n177\t  }\n178\t\n179\t  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n180\t    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth); // side view: stay on the lane\n181\t    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget; // first person: no body in the way\n182\t  }\n183\t\n184\t  status() {\n185\t    return `coins ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`;\n186\t  }\n187\t}\n188\t\n189\t/** A fresh game: the page starts one, and `node check.mjs --headless` plays one in Node (section 8). */\n190\texport const createGame = () => new CoinGarden();\n191\t\n192\tif (typeof document !== 'undefined') await Engine.start(createGame(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso' } }));\n193\t```\n194\t\n195\tTo make it yours: change the blocks (the level), the coin spots, the camera preset, the HUD,\n196\tthe win condition. `withUrlOptions(options)` adds the review flags from the page URL on top of\n197\tyour options (`?camera=side`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?look=noir`, `?debug=1`; section 8): the camera is\n198\tmerged key by key, and with no flags your options are used as they are.\n199\t\n200\t## 4. The API by system\n201\t\n202\tEverything below comes from `./pixel-engine.js`. `API.md` has the full signatures.\n203\t\n204\t### Starting\n205\t\n206\t```js\n207\tconst engine = await Engine.start(new MyGame(), withUrlOptions({  // + review flags from the URL\n208\t  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n209\t  camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.2 },  // one preset per game (see Camera)\n210\t  look: LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes,       // optional: the art style (see Looks)\n211\t  background: PALETTE.sky,               // clear colour (default PALETTE.night)\n212\t}));\n213\tawait engine.loadGame(new Level2());     // next level: same renderer, camera, input, audio\n214\t```\n215\t\n216\tShipping options: the debug panel and the engine hotkeys (P, R, \\`, [, ], M) are off outside\n217\t`?debug=1`. Players get those options in the settings panel instead (look, pixel art, pixel size,\n218\tquality, frame rate, sound, debug info; saved per game): the ⚙ button opens it, top right\n219\t(`settings: { button: 'top-left' }` moves it; `false` hides it, for a game that opens it from its\n220\town menu with `engine.settings.open()`). A game that uses R, P or M itself moves that hotkey for\n221\t`?debug=1` with `debugKeys: { resolution: 'F2' }`. `touchButtons: [{ label: 'A', code: 'Space', hint: 'jump' }]`\n222\treplaces the on-screen buttons.\n223\t`loadGame` can be called from anywhere (a trigger, `update`); the swap happens after the\n224\tframe. It unloads the old game: scene objects, physics, triggers, particles, HUD, music and\n225\tlights are freed, so a level never leaks into the next.\n226\t\n227\t### Building a level\n228\t\n229\t- **Materials:** `toonMaterial(color)` is a cached 3-band toon material. Use palette\n230\t  colours: `ctx.palette.green` or `PALETTE.green`. The 16 names: `ink plum red orange sand\n231\t  lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`. Options: `{ map, vertexColors, rim, dissolve }`.\n232\t- **Meshes:** plain three.js (`new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(c))`).\n233\t  Set `castShadow` / `receiveShadow`. For the static level, build meshes and add\n234\t  `...mergeStaticMeshes(meshes)`: one draw call per material instead of one per block (don't\n235\t  add the sources too). Keep anything that moves separate.\n236\t- **Colliders:** `physics.addStaticBox({ position, halfExtents, rotationY?, friction? })` and\n237\t  `physics.addStaticCylinder(position, halfHeight, radius)`. One per solid block, matching\n238\t  the mesh (half extents are half the size). Anything else through `RAPIER` and\n239\t  `physics.world` (see the top-down recipe's crates).\n240\t- **Collider tags** the hero understands: `physics.tag(collider, 'climbable')`, `'pushable'`,\n241\t  `'grabbable'`, `'slippery'`, `'noLedge'`, `'noCamera'` (camera rays ignore it).\n242\t- **Models:** `const m = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb')` returns `{ scene, animations }`,\n243\t  a fresh clone each call, materials converted to toon. `m.scene.clone(true)` copies a static model.\n244\t  List models in the game's `assets` so they download while the engine starts.\n245\t- **Look layers:** `setLookLayer(object, 'actors')` puts characters and objects on the actors\n246\t  layer (children follow); everything else is environment. Looks can treat them differently.\n247\t- **Shadow blob:** `new ContactShadow(radius)`, placed every frame with\n248\t  `shadow.place(x, groundY, z, heightAboveGround)` (see the quick start).\n249\t\n250\t**Models and textures as data.** Besides GLBs, the kit has a library of models (crates, stalls,\n251\tlamps, trees, doors, weapons...) and pixel-art textures, and a format to write your own as plain\n252\tdata: `buildModel('market-stall', { palette: { cloth: 'blue' } })` gives merged toon meshes, their\n253\tcollider data and light, and `textureMap('cobbles')` a map for `toonMaterial`. `ASSETS.md` is their\n254\tguide (the format, the loop, the style rules, an example scene), `LIBRARY.md` lists the library,\n255\tand `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws what you write. Read `ASSETS.md` before you write one.\n256\t\n257\t### The hero\n258\t\n259\t`PlatformerCharacter` is a Mario-64-style controller with the full moveset and animation;\n260\t`HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG` and `HERO_CLIPS` are its model and clips:\n261\t\n262\t```js\n263\tconst model = (await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false })).scene;\n264\tsetLookLayer(model, 'actors');\n265\tctx.scene.add(model);\n266\tconst hero = new PlatformerCharacter(ctx.physics, { position: [0, 0, 0], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n267\thero.attachModel(model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, model), HERO_RIG);\n268\t// fixedUpdate: input = readMoveInput(ctx, hero, input); hero.fixedUpdate(dt, input);  (input kept, filled in place)\n269\t// update:      hero.updateVisual(model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n270\t```\n271\t\n272\tControls (`readMoveInput`, camera-relative in every preset): WASD / arrows move (Shift\n273\twalks), Space jumps (again on landing: double, triple; wall kicks), C crouches (+ Space\n274\tbackflip; running: slide, long jump; in the air: ground pound), J punch / kick / dive, F grab\n275\tand pull, Z prone, X lie down, V wave, B sit. Ledges, climbing (`climbable`), pushing\n276\t(`pushable`), slopes and stairs just work. Gamepads and touch map onto the same keys.\n277\t\n278\tThe game drives it with: `hero.teleport([x, y, z])` (feet position; resets momentum),\n279\t`hero.hurt(from)` (knocked back away from `from`, the direction from the hero to what hit it:\n280\tenemy position minus hero position; false while `hero.invulnerable > 0`),\n281\t`hero.jump('Jump')` (bounce, e.g. off an enemy), `hero.celebrate()`, `hero.lookAt = vector`\n282\t(turns the head), `hero.setLockDepth(on)`. It reports `hero.state` (`'idle'`, `'run'`,\n283\t`'jump'`, ...), `hero.grounded`, `hero.vy`, `hero.speed`, `hero.facing`, `hero.stats`\n284\t(jumps, landings, hurts, ...), `hero.feetInto(v)` and `hero.body` (its Rapier body). Its\n285\tcollider carries the `'character'` tag. For a simpler walk-and-jump capsule there is\n286\t`CharacterController`.\n287\t\n288\t### Input\n289\t\n290\t`ctx.input.isDown('KeyE')` (held), `wasPressed('KeyE')` (once, in `update`),\n291\t`consumePress('KeyE')` (once, in `fixedUpdate`: it keeps a press for 150 ms so none is lost\n292\tbetween steps), `moveAxis()` (`{ x, y }` from keys, stick or touch). Codes are\n293\t`KeyboardEvent.code` values: `KeyA`, `Space`, `ArrowUp`, `Digit1`, `ShiftLeft`.\n294\t\n295\t### Physics queries and triggers\n296\t\n297\t```js\n298\tconst t = ctx.physics.trigger({ box: [1, 1.5, 0.5] }, [x, y, z], {\n299\t  tag: 'character', once: true,                // only the hero; remove itself after the first enter\n300\t  onEnter: () => win(), onExit: () => {},\n301\t});                                            // shapes: box (half extents), sphere, capsule, cylinder\n302\tt.position.set(4, 1, 0); t.enabled = false; t.remove();\n303\tctx.physics.groundBelow(origin, 20, hero.body); // { y, distance } | null\n304\tctx.physics.castRay(from, dir, maxDistance);    // { distance, collider, normal, point } | null\n305\tctx.physics.remove(colliderOrBody);             // e.g. open a gate\n306\t```\n307\t\n308\tTriggers see dynamic and kinematic bodies (the hero, crates), not level geometry. Dynamic\n309\tbodies: `RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()` and `RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(...)` on\n310\t`ctx.physics.world`, then `ctx.physics.bind(body, mesh)` so the mesh follows it.\n311\t\n312\t### Physics toys: platforms, fields, joints, destruction, cloth\n313\t\n314\t```js\n315\tconst lift = ctx.physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased());\n316\tctx.physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.2, 0.15, 1.2), lift);\n317\tctx.physics.addMover(lift, { path: [[0, 0.15, 0], [0, 4, 0]], speed: 1.5, hold: 1 }); // the hero rides it\n318\tctx.physics.addMover(disc, { spin: [0, 0.6, 0] });           // also: swing, curve: orbit(...) / pendulum(...)\n319\tctx.physics.conveyor(beltCollider, [2, 0, 0]);               // carries crates and the hero\n320\tctx.physics.fields.add({ box: [1, 4, 1], at: [4, 4, 0], force: [0, 90, 0], falloff: true, drag: 0.5 }); // an updraft\n321\tctx.physics.explode([0, 0.5, 0], { radius: 4, impulse: 12 });\n322\thero.launch(16);                                             // a trampoline\n323\thero.shove = 1.5; hero.weight = 1;                           // push loose bodies; press dynamic floors (both 0 by default)\n324\tropeBridge(ctx, [-3, 0, 0], [3, 0, 0], { planks: 10 });      // also chain, hingeDoor, springPad, seesaw\n325\tconst breaks = new Breakables(ctx);                          // breaks.add / break / crumble; update(dt, hero.groundCollider)\n326\tconst flag = clothGrid({ width: 2, height: 1.2, cols: 12, rows: 8, origin: [0, 3, 0], pin: 'left' });\n327\tctx.scene.add(new SoftMesh(flag, toonMaterial(PALETTE.red)));  // flag.step(dt) per fixed step, mesh.sync() per frame\n328\tconst balls = new InstancedBodies(ctx.physics, ctx.scene, { shape: 'ball', size: 0.2, capacity: 500 }); // one draw call\n329\t```\n330\t\n331\tMovers are functions of their own clock (same at any frame rate). `hero.stats.crushes` counts a\n332\tplatform coming down on the hero: decide what it does. Ledges are fixed bodies only.\n333\t\n334\t### Water, weather, grass, trails and decals\n335\t\n336\t```js\n337\tconst water = new WaterSurface({ size: [14, 8], at: [0, -0.2, 0], waves: WAVES_CALM, ripples: new RippleField({ size: [14, 8], cells: [112, 64] }) });\n338\tconst floaters = new Floaters(ctx.physics.world, (x, z) => (water.covers(x, z) ? water.heightAt(x, z) : null));\n339\tapplySky(ctx.engine, skyAt(19));                              // time of day: sun, colours, background\n340\tconst rain = new Precipitation({ kind: 'rain', count: 3000 }); // also 'snow'; rain.intensity, rain.wind\n341\tconst grass = new GrassField({ area: [20, 14], at: [0, 0, 0], count: 6000 });  // grass.push(0, heroFeet, 0.8)\n342\tconst decals = new Decals(ctx.scene);                          // decals.add('scorch', at, normal, { size: 2 })\n343\tctx.scene.add(water, rain, grass, new Trail({ width: 0.3 }));  // trail.push(p, t); trail.update(t, camera)\n344\t```\n345\t\n346\tPer frame: `water.update(t)`, `rain.update(dt, focus)`, `grass.update(t)`, `decals.update(dt)`;\n347\tper fixed step: `floaters.step(dt)` and the ripple field's `step(dt)`.\n348\t\n349\t### Procedural motion and sprites\n350\t\n351\t```js\n352\tconst tail = new SpringChain({ segments: 6, length: 0.12 });   // tail.update(dt, root, [right, up, forward]); tail.points\n353\tconst squash = new Squash();                                   // squash.kick(-5); squash.update(dt); squash.scale() → [xz, y]\n354\tconst legs = new LegStepper({ legs: [{ rest: [-0.6, 0.5], partners: [1] }, { rest: [0.6, 0.5], partners: [0] }] });\n355\tlegs.update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, (x, z) => 0);             // legs.feet[i].at; knee = twoBoneIK(hip, foot, a, b, pole)\n356\tconst sheet = drawSheet({ frame: [16, 16], frames: 4 }, (g, f) => { g.fillStyle = '#ffcd75'; g.fillRect(4, 4 + (f % 2), 8, 8); });\n357\tconst sprites = new SpriteBatch(sheet, { capacity: 100 });     // ctx.scene.add(sprites); sprites.spawn(at, { size: 1 })\n358\t```\n359\t\n360\tSprites face the camera, keep square pixels and are one draw per sheet: `sprites.set(i, { at, frame, flip })`.\n361\t\n362\tA ragdoll from the hero model: `const doll = new Ragdoll(ctx.physics, model, HERO_RAGDOLL); doll.enable({ velocity })`,\n363\tthen `doll.sync()` every frame instead of the mixer; `doll.release({ ground: 0 })` hands it back (blend into `GetUp`).\n364\t\n365\t### Guards, paths, flocks, cars and bullets\n366\t\n367\t```js\n368\tconst nav = NavGrid.fromRows(['#######', '#..c..#', '#.....#', '#######'], { blocked: '#c' });\n369\tconst path = nav.path([-2, 0, -0.5], [2, 0, 0.5]);         // A* (corners only); nav.lineOfSight(ax, az, bx, bz)\n370\tconst flock = new Boids(80, { bounds: { min: [-8, 2, -8], max: [8, 5, 8] } }); // flock.step(dt); flock.pos\n371\tconst car = new Vehicle(ctx.physics, { at: [0, 1, 0] });   // car.drive({ throttle, steer, handbrake }, dt) per fixed step\n372\tconst pool = new BulletPool(3000);                         // emitter({ pattern: 'spiral', every: 0.06 }).update(pool, dt, at, aim)\n373\t```\n374\t\n375\t### Mouse picking and rewind\n376\t\n377\t```js\n378\tctx.camera.rayAt(ctx.engine.input.pointer.x, ctx.engine.input.pointer.y, origin, dir); // then ctx.physics.castRay(origin, dir, 1000)\n379\tconst rewind = new Rewind(ctx.physics, { seconds: 5 }); // rewind.trackAll(); per step: rewind.rewinding = input.isDown('KeyR')\n380\t```\n381\t\n382\t### Procedural generation\n383\t\n384\t```js\n385\tconst noise = createNoise(7); // noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05) about -1..1; noise.ridged2 for crests\n386\tconst land = new Terrain({ size: [40, 30] }).generate((x, z) => 4 * noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05)); // land.erode(); land.attach(ctx.physics); ctx.scene.add(land.mesh)\n387\tconst wfc = new Wfc(patternTiles([{ pattern: ['#.#', '#.#', '#.#'] }, { pattern: ['###', '###', '###'] }]), 10, 8, { seed: 3 }); // wfc.run(); wfc.result\n388\tconst plant = turtle(expand(PLANTS.fern.system, 5), PLANTS.fern.turtle); // plant.branches: { from, to, radius }, plant.leaves\n389\t```\n390\t\n391\t### GPU compute, monitors and mirrors\n392\t\n393\t```js\n394\tconst swarm = new GpuSwarm({ count: 32768 }); ctx.scene.add(swarm.mesh); // per frame: swarm.update(ctx.engine.renderer, dt)\n395\tconst cam = new RenderView({ size: [160, 90] }); const stopCam = cam.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene); // screen.material = cam.screenMaterial(); RenderView.screen(screen)\n396\tconst mirror = new Mirror({ size: [6, 3] }); ctx.scene.add(mirror.mesh); const stopMirror = mirror.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene);\n397\t// in dispose(): stopCam(); stopMirror(); cam.dispose(); mirror.dispose(); swarm.dispose() (a level unload also drops the listeners)\n398\tconst holo = hologramMaterial({ color: PALETTE.cyan }); // forceFieldMaterial, lavaMaterial, marbleMaterial, woodMaterial, crystalMaterial\n399\t```\n400\t\n401\t### Behaviour trees, cutscenes, sound in space\n402\t\n403\t```js\n404\tconst tree = new BehaviorTree(bt.selector('npc', bt.sequence('flee', bt.condition('near?', (b) => b.near), bt.action('run', run)), bt.action('wander', wander)), board); // tree.tick(dt) per step\n405\tconst scene = new Timeline({ shots: [{ at: 0, position: [0, 6, 10], target: [0, 1, 0] }, { at: 4, position: [-1, 2, 4], target: [0, 1, 0] }], cues: [{ at: 1, run: openDoor }], lines: [{ at: 0.5, until: 3, text: 'HELLO.' }] }); // scene.play(); scene.update(dt); scene.camera(); scene.skip()\n406\tconst hum = ctx.audio.loop('coin', { volume: 0 }); // per frame: const s = spatialize({ position: ears, right }, at, { max: 20 }); hum.set({ volume: s.gain, pan: s.pan, muffle: blocked ? 0.85 : 0 })\n407\t```\n408\t\n409\t### Camera\n410\t\n411\t| Preset | View | Notes |\n412\t| --- | --- | --- |\n413\t| `iso` | orthographic 3/4 view, 32° pitch, 45° yaw | the default; `pitch`, `yaw` configurable |\n414\t| `topdown` | straight down, screen up = −Z | |\n415\t| `side` | straight side-on, screen right = +X | `ctx.camera.lockDepth` is true: pass it to the hero |\n416\t| `third` | perspective orbit behind the player | drag or Q/E to orbit |\n417\t| `first` | first person, mouse look (click locks the pointer) | `ctx.camera.hidesTarget`: hide your model |\n418\t| `free`, `fixed` | authoring fly-cam; a frozen view | `{ preset: 'fixed', position, target, fov }` |\n419\t\n420\tConfig: `{ preset, zoom, minZoom, maxZoom, viewHeight, pitch, yaw, fov, distance, stiffness }`.\n421\tPlayers zoom with the wheel or + / -. `engine.setCamera(config, { syncUrl: false })` swaps\n422\tthe preset during play (a cutscene, a minigame); the game's `onCameraChange` fixes up\n423\tanything preset-specific.\n424\t\n425\t### HUD\n426\t\n427\tA pixel canvas over the game, in art pixels, drawn after the filters (always crisp).\n428\tIt is retained: `clear()` then redraw each frame.\n429\t\n430\t```js\n431\tctx.hud.clear();\n432\tctx.hud.text(6, 6, `SCORE ${score}`, { color: 'sand' });            // 5×7 font, UPPERCASE\n433\tctx.hud.text(6, 6, 'LIVES 3', { anchor: 'top-right' });              // anchors: top-left (default), top,\n434\tctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });        //   top-right, left, center, right, bottom-*\n435\tctx.hud.rect(0, 0, ctx.hud.width, 12, 'ink', 'bottom-left');\n436\tctx.hud.sprite(6, 20, ['.oo.', 'oyyo', '.oo.'], { o: 'orange', y: 'sand' }); // '.' is transparent\n437\t```\n438\t\n439\t### Audio\n440\t\n441\t```js\n442\tctx.audio.play('coin');                       // built-in: jump doubleJump land coin step skid punch groundPound whoosh hurt fanfare\n443\tctx.audio.play('coin', { pitch: 5, volume: 0.6, pan: -0.3 });\n444\tctx.audio.register('zap', { wave: 'square', duty: 0.25, freq: 1200, freqEnd: 200, decay: 0.15, volume: 0.3 });\n445\tctx.audio.playMusic(SONG);                    // a Song (data, see the top-down recipe); playMusic(null) stops\n446\tawait ctx.audio.load('door', 'assets/door.ogg');\n447\t```\n448\t\n449\tSounds are synthesised from data, so a game needs no audio files. Browsers start sound on\n450\tthe first key or tap; before that, calls are silent (and still counted in `ctx.audio.counts`).\n451\t\n452\t### Particles and lights\n453\t\n454\t```js\n455\tctx.particles.burst('dust', at);              // built-in: dust skid sparkle smoke impact\n456\tctx.particles.burst('sparkle', at, { count: 20, colors: ['white', 'cyan'] });\n457\tctx.particles.register('ember', { count: [3, 5], life: [0.6, 1], speed: [1, 2], gravity: -2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['sand', 'orange', 'red'] });\n458\t\n459\tconst torch = ctx.lights.request({ position: [3, 1.6, 4], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 6, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\n460\tctx.lights.request({ follow: heroModel, offset: [0, 1.4, 0], intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 });\n461\ttorch.release();\n462\tctx.engine.sun.intensity = 0.6;               // the sun and ambient light are the engine's: dim them for night\n463\t```\n464\t\n465\tParticle sizes are whole art pixels and colours are palette names. Point lights come from a\n466\tfixed pool (4 to 16 by quality), handed to the most important requests: never add your own\n467\t`PointLight`s (each one recompiles every material).\n468\t\n469\t### Game feel and scene changes\n470\t\n471\t```js\n472\tctx.engine.hitstop(0.08);                    // freeze the game 0.08 s when a hit lands (rendering goes on)\n473\tctx.engine.shake.add(0.4);                   // screen shake: trauma 0..1 that drains; whole art pixels\n474\tctx.engine.timeScale = 0.25;                 // slow motion (1 normal, 0 a frozen menu); loadGame resets it\n475\tctx.engine.screen.flash('white', { duration: 0.1 });\n476\tctx.engine.screen.shockwave(blastAt, { radius: 0.4 });        // a ring that pushes the picture outward\n477\tawait ctx.engine.screen.cover('iris', { center: heroAt });    // fade iris diamonds dissolve dither blinds wipe curtain mosaic\n478\tawait ctx.engine.loadGame(new Level2());\n479\tawait ctx.engine.screen.reveal('iris');\n480\tctx.tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' }); // game time, cleared on unload\n481\tctx.tweens.call(1.5, () => spawnWave());     // a timer\n482\tctx.engine.setSunDirection([1, 0.3, 0.2]);   // a low dawn sun; the next level resets it\n483\t```\n484\t\n485\tTransitions, flashes and shockwaves are uniforms at the end of the pipeline: they never\n486\tcompile a shader, work with every look, and keep running while a level loads.\n487\t\n488\t### Looks\n489\t\n490\tA `Look` sets the art style per layer: `{ scene, actors, environment }`. Each layer is\n491\t`{ pixel: { size, outline, crease } | null, filters: [...] }` (`null` renders it clean, at\n492\tfull resolution); `scene` filters apply to the whole frame on top.\n493\t\n494\t```js\n495\tengine.setLook(LOOK_PRESETS.noir);            // or Engine.start(game, { look }) / ?look=noir\n496\tengine.setLook({\n497\t  scene: [{ id: 'vignette' }],\n498\t  actors: { pixel: { size: 2, outline: 0.8, crease: 0.1 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n499\t  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4 } }] },\n500\t});\n501\t```\n502\t\n503\tNamed looks (`LOOK_PRESETS`): `none` (the default pixel art) `no_filters` `eight_bit`\n504\t`sixteen_bit` `playstation` `arcade` `handheld` `famicom` `home_computer` `vhs_rental`\n505\t`spectrum` `mac_classic` `pico` `dream` `spooky` `pixel_heroes` `pixel_world` `chunky_world`\n506\t`cel_cartoon` `cel_heroes` `retro_heroes` `spotlight` `sketchbook` `dream_world`\n507\t`handheld_heroes` `noir` `sin_city` `comic_book` `pop_art` `storybook` `neon_nights`\n508\t`heat_vision` `night_ops` `ps1_horror` `found_footage` `old_photo` `virtual_boy` `pico_world`.\n509\tFilters (`FILTERS`, each with parameters and presets, see API.md): `cel 8bit 16bit ps1\n510\tsweetie16 pico8 nes c64 zx ega cga gameboy gbpocket virtualboy onebit dither posterize adjust\n511\tgrayscale sepia invert bleach sunset moonlight thermal nightvision scanlines lcd crt vignette\n512\tchromatic grain vhs ntsc bloom halftone sketch`. Changing parameters or pixel sizes is free;\n513\tchanging which filters are on rebuilds the pipeline once.\n514\t\n515\t## 5. Recipes\n516\t\n517\tEach recipe is a whole game, built into the kit as `examples/<name>.html`. Steal from them.\n518\t\n519\t### Side-scroller: levels as data, enemies, level progression\n520\t\n521\t```js side-scroller\n522\t// Cliff Run: a side-scroller. Run (A/D or arrows), jump (Space; again on landing for a higher\n523\t// one), stomp the slimes, reach the flag. Two levels, then it starts over.\n524\timport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PALETTE, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n525\t\n526\tconst { BoxGeometry, CylinderGeometry, Group, Mesh, SphereGeometry, Vector3 } = THREE;\n527\t\n528\t// Levels are data. platforms: [left x, right x, top y]; slimes: [x, patrol half-width]; flag: x.\n529\tconst LEVELS = [\n530\t  { platforms: [[-4, 10, 0], [12, 15, 1.2], [17, 20, 2.2], [22, 32, 0], [34, 37, 1.4], [39, 48, 0]], slimes: [[27, 3], [43, 2]], flag: 46 },\n531\t  { platforms: [[-4, 6, 0], [8, 10, 1.4], [12, 14, 2.6], [16, 18, 1.4], [20, 28, 0], [30, 31.5, 1.2], [33.5, 35, 2.2], [37, 48, 0]], slimes: [[24, 3], [41, 2]], flag: 46 },\n532\t];\n533\tconst START = [-2, 0, 0];\n534\t\n535\tclass CliffRun {\n536\t  name = 'Cliff Run';\n537\t  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/tree.glb'];\n538\t  slimes = [];\n539\t  stomps = 0;\n540\t  falls = 0;\n541\t  clearedAt = null;\n542\t  leaving = false;\n543\t  startTime = null;\n544\t  feet = new Vector3();\n545\t  from = new Vector3();\n546\t  target = new Vector3();\n547\t\n548\t  constructor(level = 0) {\n549\t    this.level = level;\n550\t    this.data = LEVELS[level];\n551\t  }\n552\t\n553\t  async setup(ctx) {\n554\t    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n555\t    const parts = [];\n556\t    for (const [x0, x1, top] of this.data.platforms) {\n557\t      const w = x1 - x0;\n558\t      const h = top + 4; // every platform reaches down out of view\n559\t      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [(x0 + x1) / 2, top - h / 2, 0], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, 2] });\n560\t      const body = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, 4), toonMaterial(palette.sand));\n561\t      body.position.set((x0 + x1) / 2, top - h / 2, 0);\n562\t      const grass = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w + 0.1, 0.3, 4.1), toonMaterial(palette.green));\n563\t      grass.position.set((x0 + x1) / 2, top - 0.14, 0);\n564\t      for (const m of [body, grass]) m.receiveShadow = m.castShadow = true;\n565\t      parts.push(body, grass);\n566\t    }\n567\t    // The flag: no collider, a trigger ends the level.\n568\t    const pole = new Mesh(new CylinderGeometry(0.06, 0.06, 3, 6), toonMaterial(palette.white));\n569\t    pole.position.set(this.data.flag, 1.5, -1);\n570\t    const cloth = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.9, 0.6, 0.06), toonMaterial(palette.red));\n571\t    cloth.position.set(this.data.flag + 0.45, 2.65, -1);\n572\t    parts.push(pole, cloth);\n573\t    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n574\t    physics.trigger({ box: [0.5, 2, 2] }, [this.data.flag, 2, 0], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.clear(ctx) });\n575\t\n576\t    // Scenery behind the lane: trees (a built-in model) and clouds.\n577\t    const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb');\n578\t    for (const x of [2, 25, 41]) {\n579\t      const t = tree.scene.clone(true);\n580\t      t.position.set(x, 0, -1.4);\n581\t      scene.add(t);\n582\t    }\n583\t    for (const [x, y] of [[0, 7], [14, 8], [30, 6.5], [44, 7.5]]) {\n584\t      const cloud = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(3, 0.8, 0.8), toonMaterial(palette.white));\n585\t      cloud.position.set(x, y, -6);\n586\t      scene.add(cloud);\n587\t    }\n588\t\n589\t    // Slimes: a squashed sphere with eyes, patrolling; stomp them from above.\n590\t    for (const [x, range] of this.data.slimes) {\n591\t      const mesh = new Group();\n592\t      const blob = new Mesh(new SphereGeometry(0.45, 12, 8), toonMaterial(palette.lime));\n593\t      blob.scale.y = 0.7;\n594\t      blob.position.y = 0.3;\n595\t      blob.castShadow = true;\n596\t      mesh.add(blob);\n597\t      for (const ex of [-0.15, 0.15]) {\n598\t        const eye = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.1, 0.14, 0.05), toonMaterial(palette.ink));\n599\t        eye.position.set(ex, 0.42, 0.4);\n600\t        mesh.add(eye);\n601\t      }\n602\t      mesh.position.set(x, 0, 0);\n603\t      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors');\n604\t      scene.add(mesh);\n605\t      this.slimes.push({ mesh, x, range, phase: x, dead: false });\n606\t    }\n607\t\n608\t    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n609\t    this.model = hero.scene;\n610\t    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n611\t    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n612\t    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n613\t    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: START, lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n614\t    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n615\t  }\n616\t\n617\t  clear(ctx) {\n618\t    this.clearedAt = ctx.time;\n619\t    this.hero.celebrate();\n620\t    ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n621\t    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', [this.data.flag, 3, -1], { count: 30 });\n622\t  }\n623\t\n624\t  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n625\t    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n626\t    if (this.clearedAt !== null) this.input.move.set(0, 0, 0); // level clear: stand still and celebrate\n627\t    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n628\t    const f = this.hero.feetInto(this.feet);\n629\t    for (const s of this.slimes) {\n630\t      if (s.dead) continue;\n631\t      s.phase += dt * 1.3;\n632\t      s.mesh.position.x = s.x + Math.sin(s.phase) * s.range;\n633\t      const dx = f.x - s.mesh.position.x;\n634\t      if (Math.abs(dx) > 0.7 || f.y > 0.9 || f.y < -0.3) continue;\n635\t      if (this.hero.vy < 0 && f.y > 0.3) {\n636\t        // landed on it: stomp and bounce\n637\t        s.dead = true;\n638\t        s.mesh.visible = false;\n639\t        this.stomps++;\n640\t        ctx.audio.play('punch');\n641\t        ctx.particles.burst('impact', s.mesh.position);\n642\t        this.hero.jump('Jump');\n643\t      } else if (this.hero.hurt(this.from.set(-dx, 0, 0))) {\n644\t        // `from` points from the hero to what hit it (slime - hero): knocked back the other way\n645\t        ctx.audio.play('hurt');\n646\t      }\n647\t    }\n648\t    if (f.y < -8) {\n649\t      this.falls++;\n650\t      ctx.audio.play('hurt');\n651\t      this.hero.teleport(START);\n652\t    }\n653\t  }\n654\t\n655\t  update(ctx, dt) {\n656\t    this.startTime ??= ctx.time;\n657\t    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n658\t    const feet = this.model.position;\n659\t    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.target.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n660\t    this.shadow.visible = !!ground;\n661\t    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n662\t    if (this.clearedAt !== null && ctx.time - this.clearedAt > 2.5 && !this.leaving) {\n663\t      this.leaving = true;\n664\t      void ctx.engine.loadGame(new CliffRun((this.level + 1) % LEVELS.length));\n665\t    }\n666\t    const time = (this.clearedAt ?? ctx.time) - this.startTime;\n667\t    ctx.hud.clear();\n668\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LEVEL ${this.level + 1}`, { color: 'sand' });\n669\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 16, `TIME ${time.toFixed(1)}  STOMPS ${this.stomps}`);\n670\t    if (this.clearedAt !== null) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'LEVEL CLEAR!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n671\t  }\n672\t\n673\t  cameraTarget() {\n674\t    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.feet.set(...START);\n675\t    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 1.2, p.z);\n676\t  }\n677\t\n678\t  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n679\t    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth);\n680\t    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget;\n681\t  }\n682\t\n683\t  status() {\n684\t    return `level ${this.level + 1}${this.clearedAt !== null ? ' clear' : ''}, stomps ${this.stomps}, falls ${this.falls}`;\n685\t  }\n686\t}\n687\t\n688\tawait Engine.start(new CliffRun(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 }, background: PALETTE.sky }));\n689\t```\n690\t\n691\t### Top-down at night: lights, pushable crates, music, custom particles\n692\t\n693\t```js top-down\n694\t// Lantern Night: walk (WASD / arrows), push both crates onto the glowing pads (walk into a\n695\t// crate to push it), then leave through the gate in the north wall.\n696\timport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, LOOK_PRESETS, mergeStaticMeshes, PALETTE, PlatformerCharacter, RAPIER, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n697\t\n698\tconst { BoxGeometry, Color, CylinderGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n699\t\n700\tconst SONG = {\n701\t  bpm: 84,\n702\t  tracks: { lead: { wave: 'triangle', decay: 0.3, volume: 0.12 }, bass: { wave: 'square', duty: 0.125, decay: 0.25, volume: 0.05 } },\n703\t  patterns: { a: { lead: 'A4 . C5 . E5 . . . D5 . C5 . B4 . . .', bass: 'A2 . . . . . . . F2 . . . . . . .' } },\n704\t  order: ['a'],\n705\t};\n706\tconst PADS = [[-3, -4], [3, -4]];\n707\tconst CRATES = [[-3, 1], [3, 1]];\n708\t\n709\tclass LanternNight {\n710\t  name = 'Lantern Night';\n711\t  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/tree.glb'];\n712\t  crates = [];\n713\t  pads = [];\n714\t  solved = false;\n715\t  escaped = false;\n716\t  nextFirefly = 0;\n717\t  target = new Vector3();\n718\t  at = new Vector3();\n719\t\n720\t  async setup(ctx) {\n721\t    const { scene, physics, palette, engine } = ctx;\n722\t    this.sparkle = ctx.random.fork('fireflies'); // drawn per frame (cosmetic): its own stream, so gameplay's never depends on the frame rate\n723\t    // Night: dim the engine's sun and ambient light (reset for the next game on unload).\n724\t    engine.sun.color.setHex(PALETTE.sky);\n725\t    engine.sun.intensity = 1.2;\n726\t    engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy);\n727\t    engine.ambient.intensity = 1.4;\n728\t    scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.ink);\n729\t\n730\t    const parts = [];\n731\t    const wall = (x, z, w, d) => {\n732\t      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, 0.6, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, 0.6, d / 2] });\n733\t      const m = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, 1.2, d), toonMaterial(palette.slate));\n734\t      m.position.set(x, 0.6, z);\n735\t      m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n736\t      parts.push(m);\n737\t    };\n738\t    const floor = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(30, 1, 30), toonMaterial(palette.teal));\n739\t    floor.position.y = -0.5;\n740\t    floor.receiveShadow = true;\n741\t    parts.push(floor);\n742\t    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, -0.5, 0], halfExtents: [15, 0.5, 15] });\n743\t    wall(-4.75, -8, 6.5, 1); // north wall, with a 3 m gap for the gate\n744\t    wall(4.75, -8, 6.5, 1);\n745\t    wall(0, 8, 17, 1);\n746\t    wall(-8, 0, 1, 15);\n747\t    wall(8, 0, 1, 15);\n748\t    for (const [x, z] of [[-6.5, -6.5], [6.5, -6.5], [-6.5, 6.5], [6.5, 6.5]]) {\n749\t      const post = new Mesh(new CylinderGeometry(0.12, 0.15, 1.6, 6), toonMaterial(palette.plum));\n750\t      post.position.set(x, 0.8, z);\n751\t      parts.push(post);\n752\t      ctx.lights.request({ position: [x, 1.8, z], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 7, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\n753\t    }\n754\t    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n755\t\n756\t    // The gate: a collider we remove when the puzzle is solved.\n757\t    this.gate = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(3, 1.6, 0.6), toonMaterial(palette.red));\n758\t    this.gate.position.set(0, 0.8, -8);\n759\t    scene.add(this.gate);\n760\t    this.gateCollider = physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, 0.8, -8], halfExtents: [1.5, 0.8, 0.3] });\n761\t    physics.trigger({ box: [1.5, 1, 0.5] }, [0, 1, -9.2], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.escape(ctx) });\n762\t\n763\t    // Pads and crates. A crate is a dynamic Rapier body the hero pushes (tag 'pushable').\n764\t    for (const [x, z] of PADS) {\n765\t      const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1.2, 0.06, 1.2), toonMaterial(palette.navy));\n766\t      mesh.position.set(x, 0.03, z);\n767\t      scene.add(mesh);\n768\t      this.pads.push({ x, z, mesh, lit: false });\n769\t    }\n770\t    for (const [x, z] of CRATES) {\n771\t      const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.98, 0.98, 0.98), toonMaterial(palette.orange));\n772\t      mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n773\t      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors');\n774\t      scene.add(mesh);\n775\t      const body = physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic().setTranslation(x, 0.5, z).setLinearDamping(4).enabledRotations(false, false, false));\n776\t      physics.tag(physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(0.49, 0.49, 0.49).setFriction(0.1).setDensity(8), body), 'pushable');\n777\t      physics.bind(body, mesh);\n778\t      this.crates.push(body);\n779\t    }\n780\t\n781\t    const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb');\n782\t    for (let i = 0; i < 14; i++) {\n783\t      const a = (i / 14) * Math.PI * 2;\n784\t      const t = tree.scene.clone(true);\n785\t      t.position.set(Math.cos(a) * 11.5, 0, Math.sin(a) * 11.5);\n786\t      scene.add(t);\n787\t    }\n788\t\n789\t    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n790\t    this.model = hero.scene;\n791\t    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n792\t    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n793\t    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n794\t    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 5], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n795\t    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n796\t    ctx.lights.request({ follow: this.model, offset: [0, 1.5, 0], color: 0xffe0a0, intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 }); // the lantern\n797\t\n798\t    ctx.particles.register('firefly', { count: [1, 1], life: [1.5, 2.5], speed: [0.2, 0.5], gravity: -0.2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['lime', 'sand', 'lime'] });\n799\t    ctx.audio.register('chime', { wave: 'triangle', freq: 880, decay: 0.4, volume: 0.3, arp: [0, 4, 7], arpRate: 0.06 });\n800\t    ctx.audio.playMusic(SONG, 'night');\n801\t  }\n802\t\n803\t  escape(ctx) {\n804\t    this.escaped = true;\n805\t    this.hero.celebrate();\n806\t    ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n807\t  }\n808\t\n809\t  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n810\t    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n811\t    if (this.escaped) this.input.move.set(0, 0, 0); // the end: stand and celebrate\n812\t    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n813\t  }\n814\t\n815\t  update(ctx, dt) {\n816\t    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n817\t    const feet = this.model.position;\n818\t    this.shadow.place(feet.x, 0, feet.z, feet.y); // flat floor: the ground is y = 0\n819\t    // A pad lights when a crate sits on it; both lit opens the gate.\n820\t    for (const pad of this.pads) {\n821\t      const on = this.crates.some((b) => Math.hypot(b.translation().x - pad.x, b.translation().z - pad.z) < 0.5);\n822\t      if (on && !pad.lit) {\n823\t        pad.lit = true;\n824\t        pad.mesh.material = toonMaterial(ctx.palette.lime);\n825\t        ctx.lights.request({ position: [pad.x, 0.6, pad.z], color: 0x9cf060, intensity: 5, radius: 4, flicker: 'pulse' });\n826\t        ctx.audio.play('chime');\n827\t      }\n828\t    }\n829\t    if (!this.solved && this.pads.every((p) => p.lit)) {\n830\t      this.solved = true;\n831\t      ctx.physics.remove(this.gateCollider);\n832\t      this.gate.visible = false;\n833\t      ctx.particles.burst('smoke', this.gate.position, { count: 24 });\n834\t      ctx.audio.play('groundPound');\n835\t    }\n836\t    if (ctx.time > this.nextFirefly) {\n837\t      this.nextFirefly = ctx.time + 0.3;\n838\t      ctx.particles.burst('firefly', this.at.set(this.sparkle.range(-7, 7), this.sparkle.range(0.5, 2), this.sparkle.range(-7, 7)));\n839\t    }\n840\t    const lit = this.pads.filter((p) => p.lit).length;\n841\t    ctx.hud.clear();\n842\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, this.escaped ? 'YOU ESCAPED!' : this.solved ? 'THE GATE IS OPEN' : `PADS ${lit}/${this.pads.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n843\t  }\n844\t\n845\t  cameraTarget() {\n846\t    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 5);\n847\t    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n848\t  }\n849\t\n850\t  status() {\n851\t    return this.escaped ? 'escaped' : `pads ${this.pads.filter((p) => p.lit).length}/${this.pads.length}`;\n852\t  }\n853\t}\n854\t\n855\tawait Engine.start(new LanternNight(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'topdown', zoom: 1.1 }, look: LOOK_PRESETS.dream }));\n856\t```\n857\t\n858\t### Looks: mixing art styles per layer\n859\t\n860\t```js looks\n861\t// Look Lab: one scene, six looks. Keys 1-6 pick a look, T toggles chunky pixels on the custom\n862\t// one. Walk around (WASD): characters & objects (the 'actors' layer) and the environment each\n863\t// get their own look.\n864\timport { compileClips, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, LOOK_PRESETS, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n865\t\n866\tconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n867\t\n868\t// A look of our own: pixel-art characters on a clean, cel-shaded world, a vignette on top.\n869\tconst STORYBOOK_HEROES = {\n870\t  scene: [{ id: 'vignette', params: { intensity: 0.35 } }],\n871\t  actors: { pixel: { size: 1, outline: 0.85, crease: 0.2 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n872\t  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4, ink: 0.6 } }] },\n873\t};\n874\tconst LOOKS = [\n875\t  ['default', LOOK_PRESETS.none],\n876\t  ['storybook heroes', STORYBOOK_HEROES],\n877\t  ['pixel heroes', LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes],\n878\t  ['noir', LOOK_PRESETS.noir],\n879\t  ['sin city', LOOK_PRESETS.sin_city],\n880\t  ['handheld', LOOK_PRESETS.handheld],\n881\t];\n882\t\n883\tclass LookLab {\n884\t  name = 'Look Lab';\n885\t  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb', 'assets/tree.glb'];\n886\t  current = 1;\n887\t  target = new Vector3();\n888\t\n889\t  async setup(ctx) {\n890\t    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n891\t    const parts = [];\n892\t    for (const [x, z, w, h, d, c] of [[0, 0, 16, 1, 12, 'green'], [-4, -3, 2, 2, 2, 'slate'], [4, -3, 3, 1, 2, 'sand'], [0, -5, 6, 3, 1, 'plum']]) {\n893\t      const y = c === 'green' ? -0.5 : h / 2;\n894\t      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n895\t      const m = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(palette[c]));\n896\t      m.position.set(x, y, z);\n897\t      m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n898\t      parts.push(m);\n899\t    }\n900\t    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n901\t    for (const [x, z] of [[-6, 3], [6, 2], [-2, -4]]) {\n902\t      const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb'); // environment: no layer tag\n903\t      tree.scene.position.set(x, 0, z);\n904\t      scene.add(tree.scene);\n905\t    }\n906\t    // Objects on the actors layer: coins and a crate.\n907\t    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb');\n908\t    this.coins = [[-1.5, 0.6, 1], [0, 0.6, 1], [1.5, 0.6, 1]].map(([x, y, z]) => {\n909\t      const c = coin.scene.clone(true);\n910\t      c.position.set(x, y, z);\n911\t      setLookLayer(c, 'actors');\n912\t      scene.add(c);\n913\t      return c;\n914\t    });\n915\t    const crate = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), toonMaterial(palette.orange));\n916\t    crate.position.set(3, 0.5, 2);\n917\t    crate.castShadow = true;\n918\t    setLookLayer(crate, 'actors');\n919\t    scene.add(crate);\n920\t    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [3, 0.5, 2], halfExtents: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] });\n921\t\n922\t    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL);\n923\t    this.model = hero.scene;\n924\t    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n925\t    scene.add(this.model);\n926\t    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 3], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n927\t    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n928\t  }\n929\t\n930\t  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n931\t    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n932\t    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n933\t  }\n934\t\n935\t  update(ctx, dt) {\n936\t    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n937\t    for (const c of this.coins) c.rotation.y += dt * 2;\n938\t    for (let i = 0; i < LOOKS.length; i++) {\n939\t      if (ctx.input.wasPressed(`Digit${i + 1}`)) {\n940\t        this.current = i;\n941\t        ctx.engine.setLook(LOOKS[i][1]);\n942\t      }\n943\t    }\n944\t    if (ctx.input.wasPressed('KeyT')) {\n945\t      // Parameters and pixel sizes are uniforms: changing them never rebuilds the pipeline.\n946\t      STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size = STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size === 1 ? 3 : 1;\n947\t      if (this.current === 1) ctx.engine.setLook(STORYBOOK_HEROES);\n948\t    }\n949\t    ctx.hud.clear();\n950\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LOOK ${this.current + 1}/${LOOKS.length}: ${LOOKS[this.current][0].toUpperCase()}`, { color: 'sand' });\n951\t    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, '1-6 LOOKS  T CHUNKY', { anchor: 'bottom-left' });\n952\t  }\n953\t\n954\t  cameraTarget() {\n955\t    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 3);\n956\t    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n957\t  }\n958\t\n959\t  status() {\n960\t    return `look ${LOOKS[this.current][0]}`;\n961\t  }\n962\t}\n963\t\n964\tawait Engine.start(new LookLab(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.3 }, look: STORYBOOK_HEROES }));\n965\t```\n966\t\n967\t## 6. Rules\n968\t\n969\t1. **Import everything from `./pixel-engine.js`.** three.js is `THREE` (`three/webgpu`) and\n970\t   `TSL`; Rapier is `RAPIER`. Never load three.js or Rapier from a CDN or npm as well: two\n971\t   copies break `instanceof` checks and the renderer.\n972\t2. **Never edit `pixel-engine.js`.** Upgrade by replacing it (section 10).\n973\t3. **No WebGL-era three.js APIs.** The renderer is `WebGPURenderer`: `WebGLRenderer`,\n974\t   `EffectComposer`, `ShaderMaterial`, `onBeforeCompile` and GLSL don't work. Custom shading is\n975\t   TSL on node materials; post effects are looks.\n976\t4. **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** Move characters and apply forces at\n977\t   the fixed 60 Hz step; animate, spin pickups and draw the HUD per frame. Use `ctx.time`\n978\t   (it stops while paused) and the `dt` you are given, never `performance.now()`. Anything\n979\t   random comes from `ctx.random` (`range`, `int`, `chance`, `pick`, `fork`), never\n980\t   `Math.random`: the same seed (`?seed=`) and input then replay the same game\n981\t   (`engine.recording()`, `engine.replay()`, section 8). Draw it in `setup`, `fixedUpdate` and\n982\t   event handlers; randomness drawn per frame in `update` (cosmetic) comes from a fork made in\n983\t   `setup`, so the frame rate never changes what gameplay draws. Read buttons from `ctx.input`\n984\t   (mouse buttons are keys: `isDown('MouseLeft')`; a finger is `Touch`), and data from the page (a save in storage, a\n985\t   text field) through `ctx.input.send(data)` → `ctx.input.commands` next frame, so they replay too.\n986\t5. **Physics owns positions.** Don't set `mesh.position` on things with bodies: `physics.bind`\n987\t   them, or let the character controller pose its model. Static blocks: one collider each,\n988\t   the same size as the mesh.\n989\t6. **Palette colours and toon materials** (`toonMaterial(palette.x)`), chunky shapes (≥ 0.25 m).\n990\t   Other materials render, but break the look.\n991\t7. **Lights come from `ctx.lights`**, never `new PointLight()`. Dim `ctx.engine.sun` and\n992\t   `ctx.engine.ambient` for night scenes.\n993\t8. **Tag characters and objects** with `setLookLayer(root, 'actors')`, so looks can treat\n994\t   them apart from the environment.\n995\t9. **Reuse vectors in per-frame code** (`hero.feetInto(v)`, a `target` vector for\n996\t   `cameraTarget`): no garbage per frame.\n997\t10. **Give `status()` a meaningful line** (score, lives, level, state): the check tool and\n998\t   other agents read it.\n999\t11. **Clean up what the engine doesn't own**: DOM you add, timers and listeners go in `dispose(ctx)`.\n1000\t\n1001\t## 7. Common mistakes\n1002\t\n1003\t| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n1004\t| --- | --- | --- |\n1005\t| Blank page, console: CORS / \"Failed to load module script\" | opened with `file://` | serve the folder over HTTP |\n1006\t| Console: \"Multiple instances of Three.js being imported\" | three.js loaded twice | use `THREE` from the bundle, nothing else |\n1007\t| A red **GAME ERROR** box | an exception in a hook | the box and `engine.errors` have the message, the console the stack: fix the first one |\n1008\t| \"Failed to start: ...\" | `setup()` threw (often a bad model path or a typo'd name) | read the message; check the path or the name in API.md |\n1009\t| The hero falls through the world | no collider under the mesh | `physics.addStaticBox` for every solid block |\n1010\t| The hero is stuck at the start | spawned inside a block | `position` is the feet: put it on the top surface |\n1011\t| A trigger never fires | wrong tag, or it doesn't overlap the body | `tag: 'character'` only matches the hero; level geometry needs `includeStatic: true` |\n1012\t| A colour is black or a material missing | a colour name that isn't in the palette | the 16 names in section 4 |\n1013\t| A model 404s | path not relative to the page | `assets/x.glb` next to `index.html`; built-ins need no file |\n1014\t| HUD text shows gaps | characters the 5×7 font doesn't have | uppercase letters, digits, common punctuation |\n1015\t| A key does nothing in `fixedUpdate` | `wasPressed` there | `consumePress` in `fixedUpdate`, `wasPressed` in `update` |\n1016\t| The ⚙ sits over the HUD's top-right corner on a phone | the settings button (on a mouse it shows only while the pointer moves) | `settings: { button: 'top-left' }`, or `false` and `engine.settings.open()` from your own menu |\n1017\t| Side-scroller hero drifts off the lane | `lockDepth` not passed | `lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth` |\n1018\t| The camera doesn't follow | `cameraTarget` returns nothing before `setup` ends | return a fallback vector (see the quick start) |\n1019\t| Slow, low fps | hundreds of separate meshes | `mergeStaticMeshes` the static level; share materials (`toonMaterial` caches) |\n1020\t| Everything is dark | a dimmed sun with no lights | `ctx.lights.request(...)` torches, or raise `ctx.engine.ambient.intensity` |\n1021\t| No sound | the browser waits for a key or tap | expected: sound starts on the first input |\n1022\t\n1023\t## 8. Debugging and checking\n1024\t\n1025\tThe engine is on `window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__` once it runs (also the value `Engine.start` returns).\n1026\tA tool waits on `window.__PIXEL_READY__` (it resolves with the engine, or rejects with why the start\n1027\tfailed); a game puts its own page handles in `ready(ctx)`, which runs then and after each `loadGame`.\n1028\t\n1029\t```js\n1030\tconst e = await window.__PIXEL_READY__;   // step() before this throws\n1031\te.state();                // version, backend, fps, camera, look, game status, errors, gpuErrors, ...\n1032\te.errors;                 // exceptions thrown by the game's hooks in the render loop (also on screen)\n1033\te.step(60);               // manual time: exactly 60 frames (1 s), a hook's exception thrown to you; e.manual = false resumes\n1034\t                          // step() draws nothing, so a page screenshot still shows the old frame: read the new one with\n1035\t                          // await e.renderer.capture() (pixels, no HUD), or set e.manual = false and let a frame draw\n1036\te.input.setKey('KeyD', true); e.step(30); e.input.setKey('KeyD', false);  // play by script\n1037\tconst rec = e.recording(); const end = e.fingerprint();            // this level's input as JSON, a hash of its state\n1038\tawait e.replay(rec, () => e.loadGame(new MyGame())); e.fingerprint() === end;  // replayed: the same game\n1039\t// Engine.headless(game, { assets }): the same game in Node (below)\n1040\tconst snap = await e.snapshot(); e.step(300); await e.restore(snap);   // a save-state (Game.snapshot / restore): back at once\n1041\tawait e.renderer.capture();   // { width, height, pixels }: the exact frame (without the HUD canvas)\n1042\te.game;                   // your game object: e.game.hero.teleport([x, y, z]) to test a spot\n1043\te.setLook(e.lookPresets.noir); e.setCamera({ preset: 'third' }, { syncUrl: false });\n1044\te.audio.counts; e.particles.alive; e.physics.counts(); e.hud.canvas;\n1045\t```\n1046\t\n1047\tURL flags (with `withUrlOptions` in `Engine.start`): `?debug=1` (debug panel),\n1048\t`?look=noir`, `?filters=crt,scanlines`,\n1049\t`?camera=third`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?mode=raw` (no pixel pass), `?res=320`, `?touch=1`,\n1050\t`?quality=low`, `?fps=30`, `?seed=7` (`ctx.random`); they win over the player's saved settings\n1051\t(`e.settings.values`). Hotkeys, with `?debug=1`: P pixel/raw, R resolution, \\` debug panel, [ and ]\n1052\tcycle looks, M mute.\n1053\t\n1054\t**`check.mjs` plays a page in a real browser** and fails on anything wrong:\n1055\t\n1056\t```\n1057\tnode check.mjs                                  # index.html: start, run 90 frames, screenshot\n1058\tnode check.mjs --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyD*40   # hold each key for that many frames, in order\n1059\tnode check.mjs examples/side-scroller.html --cameras --looks noir,handheld\n1060\tnode check.mjs \"index.html?look=handheld&zoom=1.5\"   # with URL flags\n1061\tnode check.mjs --sheets models.js               # sheets of your own models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n1062\t```\n1063\t\n1064\tIt plays on WebGPU in Chromium's headless shell, so it needs no display. It writes\n1065\t`check/<page>.png` (the frame with the HUD), the camera and look shots and\n1066\t`check/report.json` (errors, warnings, `state()`), and prints a summary: the backend, frames\n1067\tand your `status()`. Exit code 0: the game started, ran with no page, console, game or GPU\n1068\terrors and drew a real picture; 1: it didn't; 2: the tool couldn't run (a browser without\n1069\tWebGPU, bad arguments, no page, no browser; `--help` lists the options). It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and Playwright\n1070\t(`npm i -D playwright && npx playwright install chromium`; a global install works too), or\n1071\t`playwright-core` plus a Chromium in `CHROMIUM_PATH`. **Look at the PNGs**: a passing check only proves nothing crashed.\n1072\t\n1073\t**Play it in Node.** `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` runs a game as `Engine.start` does, minus\n1074\tthe GPU and DOM, on manual time, from the kit alone; `assets` reads the game's files.\n1075\t`node check.mjs --headless game.js` plays its `createGame()` (the starter's) for the `--keys`,\n1076\treplays it in a fresh game and fails on an error or a replay that ends elsewhere. By hand:\n1077\t\n1078\t```js\n1079\t// test.mjs, beside game.js\n1080\timport { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\n1081\timport { Engine } from './pixel-engine.js';\n1082\timport { createGame } from './game.js';\n1083\t\n1084\tconst assets = async (path) => new Uint8Array(await readFile(new URL(path, import.meta.url))).buffer;\n1085\tconst engine = await Engine.headless(createGame(), { assets });\n1086\tengine.step(60); // input.setKey to play; a hook's error throws\n1087\tconst end = engine.fingerprint();\n1088\tawait engine.replay(engine.recording(), () => engine.loadGame(createGame()));\n1089\tconsole.log(engine.state().status, engine.fingerprint() === end);\n1090\t```\n1091\t\n1092\tCode that touches `document` guards it, as the starter does.\n1093\t\n1094\t## 9. Before you hand back\n1095\t\n1096\t- [ ] `node check.mjs` exits 0 for every page, with `--keys` that actually play the game\n1097\t      (reach a goal, collect something, lose a life).\n1098\t- [ ] `node check.mjs --headless game.js` exits 0 with the same `--keys`.\n1099\t- [ ] You looked at the screenshots: the scene reads, the HUD is legible, nothing is black or\n1100\t      clipped, the hero is on the ground.\n1101\t- [ ] `status()` reports the game's state, and it changes as you play.\n1102\t- [ ] No WebGL-era APIs, no second three.js, no `new PointLight`, no unknown palette names.\n1103\t- [ ] The controls are written down (the header comment of `game.js`, or on screen).\n1104\t- [ ] On a phone (or `?touch=1`) the game is playable with the touch buttons, or you set\n1105\t      `touchButtons` for its own actions.\n1106\t\n1107\t## 10. Versions\n1108\t\n1109\t`ENGINE_VERSION` (also `Engine.version` and `state().version`) is the engine's version, and\n1110\tthe kit's `CHANGELOG.md` says what changed in each. To upgrade a game, replace\n1111\t`pixel-engine.js` (and `pixel-engine.d.ts`, `types/` if you use them), read the changelog\n1112\tentries since the old version, then run `node check.mjs` again. The newest release of the kit is\n1113\tat the URL in `manifest.json`'s `latest`, and every release (from 0.39.0, its changelog as the\n1114\tnotes) stays downloadable from `releases`. The kit's `README.md` says how to build\n1115\tit from the engine's repository (`source`) instead.\n1116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"41ea54c0-1693-4a73-8ca5-d19cf07f2e5a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:34.137Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/engine/x/pixel-engine/GUIDE.md","content":"# Pixel Engine: the guide\n\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\n\nThis guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\n`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\nwritten to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\nneed a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\n\nContents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\n6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\n\n## 1. Host it\n\nA game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\n\n```\nmy-game/\n  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\n  index.html          a page with <div id=\"app\"> that loads game.js as a module\n  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\n  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\n```\n\nThe kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\nSplit a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\n\n- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\n  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\n- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\n  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\n  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\n  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\n- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\n  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\n  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\n- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\n  browser without it is told so (\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\"). Phones and tablets\n  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\n- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\n  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\n  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\n  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\n\n## 2. Mental model\n\n**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\nshapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\nresolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\nup by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\npipeline. A **look** can change that: other palettes, CRT, cel shading, or clean\nfull-resolution rendering, per layer (section 4, *Looks*).\n\n**The engine owns the machinery; a game is a plain object with hooks.** The engine owns the\nrenderer, camera rig, physics stepping, the sun and ambient light, input, audio, particles,\nthe pixel HUD, the loading screen and the debug tools. You write a class:\n\n| Hook | When | Do here |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `name`, `assets` | before start | the title; models to start downloading early |\n| `async setup(ctx)` | once | build the level: meshes, colliders, models, characters, triggers |\n| `fixedUpdate(ctx, dt)` | 60 times a second, before each physics step | movement, forces, anything gameplay that must be exact |\n| `update(ctx, dt)` | every rendered frame, after physics | animation, visuals, pickups, HUD |\n| `cameraTarget(ctx)` | every frame | return the `Vector3` the camera follows |\n| `status(ctx)` | tooling | a one-line summary (`'coins 3/4'`), shown by the check tool |\n| `fingerprint(ctx)` | tooling | the game's own state as a string (positions, counters), part of `engine.fingerprint()` so a replay can be compared |\n| `snapshot(ctx)`, `restore(ctx, state)`, `beforeRestore(ctx)` | save-states | the game's own state as plain data (as it is, not through JSON), and back (`await engine.snapshot()`, `await engine.restore(snap)`); physics bodies are the engine's: keep their handles; `beforeRestore` leaves a mode with its own camera first |\n| `onCameraChange(ctx)`, `eyePosition(ctx)`, `dispose(ctx)` | optional | camera swaps, first-person eye, cleanup |\n| `ready(ctx)` | optional: when the engine starts running the game, and after each `loadGame` | page handles for tools (`window.__MYGAME__`) |\n\n`ctx` (the `GameContext`) is the same object in every hook: `ctx.scene` (three.js scene),\n`ctx.physics`, `ctx.input`, `ctx.camera`, `ctx.audio`, `ctx.particles`, `ctx.hud`,\n`ctx.lights`, `ctx.palette`, `ctx.loadModel`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.random` and `ctx.engine`.\n\n**Physics owns positions; visuals follow.** Bodies move in fixed 60 Hz steps (Rapier).\nMeshes are interpolated between steps, so motion is smooth at any frame rate. A character\ncontroller moves its body in `fixedUpdate`; its model is posed in `update`.\n\n**Scale.** One unit is one metre, Y is up, the ground is usually at y = 0. The hero is 1.6 m\ntall, runs at 6.5 m/s and jumps 1.7 m (double jump 2.4 m, triple 3.5 m; it also grabs ledges).\nAt the default zoom the camera shows about 13.5 m vertically, so one art pixel is about\n5 cm: give shapes at least 0.25 m so they read.\n\n## 3. Quick start\n\nA complete game, and the kit's `game.js`. Walk with WASD or the arrows, jump with Space,\ncollect the four coins.\n\n```js quickstart\n// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n  return mesh;\n}\n\nclass CoinGarden {\n  name = 'Coin Garden';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\n  coins = [];\n  collected = 0;\n  won = false;\n  target = new Vector3();\n  ground = new Vector3();\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\n    scene.add(\n      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\n        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\n        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\n        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\n        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\n      ]),\n    );\n    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\n    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\n      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\n      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      const item = { mesh, taken: false };\n      physics.trigger({ cylinder: { halfHeight: 0.6, radius: 0.5 } }, [x, y, z], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.collect(ctx, item) });\n      this.coins.push(item);\n    }\n    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 4], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n  }\n\n  collect(ctx, item) {\n    item.taken = true;\n    item.mesh.visible = false;\n    this.collected++;\n    ctx.audio.play('coin');\n    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', item.mesh.position);\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input); // filled in place after the first step\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input); // movement and forces: fixed 60 Hz step\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha); // pose and interpolate\n    const feet = this.model.position;\n    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.ground.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n    for (const c of this.coins) if (!c.taken) c.mesh.rotation.y += dt * 3;\n    if (!this.won && this.collected === this.coins.length) {\n      this.won = true;\n      this.hero.celebrate();\n      ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n    }\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 4);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n  }\n\n  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth); // side view: stay on the lane\n    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget; // first person: no body in the way\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return `coins ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`;\n  }\n}\n\n/** A fresh game: the page starts one, and `node check.mjs --headless` plays one in Node (section 8). */\nexport const createGame = () => new CoinGarden();\n\nif (typeof document !== 'undefined') await Engine.start(createGame(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso' } }));\n```\n\nTo make it yours: change the blocks (the level), the coin spots, the camera preset, the HUD,\nthe win condition. `withUrlOptions(options)` adds the review flags from the page URL on top of\nyour options (`?camera=side`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?look=noir`, `?debug=1`; section 8): the camera is\nmerged key by key, and with no flags your options are used as they are.\n\n## 4. The API by system\n\nEverything below comes from `./pixel-engine.js`. `API.md` has the full signatures.\n\n### Starting\n\n```js\nconst engine = await Engine.start(new MyGame(), withUrlOptions({  // + review flags from the URL\n  container: document.getElementById('app'),\n  camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.2 },  // one preset per game (see Camera)\n  look: LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes,       // optional: the art style (see Looks)\n  background: PALETTE.sky,               // clear colour (default PALETTE.night)\n}));\nawait engine.loadGame(new Level2());     // next level: same renderer, camera, input, audio\n```\n\nShipping options: the debug panel and the engine hotkeys (P, R, \\`, [, ], M) are off outside\n`?debug=1`. Players get those options in the settings panel instead (look, pixel art, pixel size,\nquality, frame rate, sound, debug info; saved per game): the ⚙ button opens it, top right\n(`settings: { button: 'top-left' }` moves it; `false` hides it, for a game that opens it from its\nown menu with `engine.settings.open()`). A game that uses R, P or M itself moves that hotkey for\n`?debug=1` with `debugKeys: { resolution: 'F2' }`. `touchButtons: [{ label: 'A', code: 'Space', hint: 'jump' }]`\nreplaces the on-screen buttons.\n`loadGame` can be called from anywhere (a trigger, `update`); the swap happens after the\nframe. It unloads the old game: scene objects, physics, triggers, particles, HUD, music and\nlights are freed, so a level never leaks into the next.\n\n### Building a level\n\n- **Materials:** `toonMaterial(color)` is a cached 3-band toon material. Use palette\n  colours: `ctx.palette.green` or `PALETTE.green`. The 16 names: `ink plum red orange sand\n  lime green teal navy blue sky cyan white mist slate night`. Options: `{ map, vertexColors, rim, dissolve }`.\n- **Meshes:** plain three.js (`new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(c))`).\n  Set `castShadow` / `receiveShadow`. For the static level, build meshes and add\n  `...mergeStaticMeshes(meshes)`: one draw call per material instead of one per block (don't\n  add the sources too). Keep anything that moves separate.\n- **Colliders:** `physics.addStaticBox({ position, halfExtents, rotationY?, friction? })` and\n  `physics.addStaticCylinder(position, halfHeight, radius)`. One per solid block, matching\n  the mesh (half extents are half the size). Anything else through `RAPIER` and\n  `physics.world` (see the top-down recipe's crates).\n- **Collider tags** the hero understands: `physics.tag(collider, 'climbable')`, `'pushable'`,\n  `'grabbable'`, `'slippery'`, `'noLedge'`, `'noCamera'` (camera rays ignore it).\n- **Models:** `const m = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb')` returns `{ scene, animations }`,\n  a fresh clone each call, materials converted to toon. `m.scene.clone(true)` copies a static model.\n  List models in the game's `assets` so they download while the engine starts.\n- **Look layers:** `setLookLayer(object, 'actors')` puts characters and objects on the actors\n  layer (children follow); everything else is environment. Looks can treat them differently.\n- **Shadow blob:** `new ContactShadow(radius)`, placed every frame with\n  `shadow.place(x, groundY, z, heightAboveGround)` (see the quick start).\n\n**Models and textures as data.** Besides GLBs, the kit has a library of models (crates, stalls,\nlamps, trees, doors, weapons...) and pixel-art textures, and a format to write your own as plain\ndata: `buildModel('market-stall', { palette: { cloth: 'blue' } })` gives merged toon meshes, their\ncollider data and light, and `textureMap('cobbles')` a map for `toonMaterial`. `ASSETS.md` is their\nguide (the format, the loop, the style rules, an example scene), `LIBRARY.md` lists the library,\nand `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws what you write. Read `ASSETS.md` before you write one.\n\n### The hero\n\n`PlatformerCharacter` is a Mario-64-style controller with the full moveset and animation;\n`HERO_MODEL`, `HERO_RIG` and `HERO_CLIPS` are its model and clips:\n\n```js\nconst model = (await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false })).scene;\nsetLookLayer(model, 'actors');\nctx.scene.add(model);\nconst hero = new PlatformerCharacter(ctx.physics, { position: [0, 0, 0], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\nhero.attachModel(model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, model), HERO_RIG);\n// fixedUpdate: input = readMoveInput(ctx, hero, input); hero.fixedUpdate(dt, input);  (input kept, filled in place)\n// update:      hero.updateVisual(model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n```\n\nControls (`readMoveInput`, camera-relative in every preset): WASD / arrows move (Shift\nwalks), Space jumps (again on landing: double, triple; wall kicks), C crouches (+ Space\nbackflip; running: slide, long jump; in the air: ground pound), J punch / kick / dive, F grab\nand pull, Z prone, X lie down, V wave, B sit. Ledges, climbing (`climbable`), pushing\n(`pushable`), slopes and stairs just work. Gamepads and touch map onto the same keys.\n\nThe game drives it with: `hero.teleport([x, y, z])` (feet position; resets momentum),\n`hero.hurt(from)` (knocked back away from `from`, the direction from the hero to what hit it:\nenemy position minus hero position; false while `hero.invulnerable > 0`),\n`hero.jump('Jump')` (bounce, e.g. off an enemy), `hero.celebrate()`, `hero.lookAt = vector`\n(turns the head), `hero.setLockDepth(on)`. It reports `hero.state` (`'idle'`, `'run'`,\n`'jump'`, ...), `hero.grounded`, `hero.vy`, `hero.speed`, `hero.facing`, `hero.stats`\n(jumps, landings, hurts, ...), `hero.feetInto(v)` and `hero.body` (its Rapier body). Its\ncollider carries the `'character'` tag. For a simpler walk-and-jump capsule there is\n`CharacterController`.\n\n### Input\n\n`ctx.input.isDown('KeyE')` (held), `wasPressed('KeyE')` (once, in `update`),\n`consumePress('KeyE')` (once, in `fixedUpdate`: it keeps a press for 150 ms so none is lost\nbetween steps), `moveAxis()` (`{ x, y }` from keys, stick or touch). Codes are\n`KeyboardEvent.code` values: `KeyA`, `Space`, `ArrowUp`, `Digit1`, `ShiftLeft`.\n\n### Physics queries and triggers\n\n```js\nconst t = ctx.physics.trigger({ box: [1, 1.5, 0.5] }, [x, y, z], {\n  tag: 'character', once: true,                // only the hero; remove itself after the first enter\n  onEnter: () => win(), onExit: () => {},\n});                                            // shapes: box (half extents), sphere, capsule, cylinder\nt.position.set(4, 1, 0); t.enabled = false; t.remove();\nctx.physics.groundBelow(origin, 20, hero.body); // { y, distance } | null\nctx.physics.castRay(from, dir, maxDistance);    // { distance, collider, normal, point } | null\nctx.physics.remove(colliderOrBody);             // e.g. open a gate\n```\n\nTriggers see dynamic and kinematic bodies (the hero, crates), not level geometry. Dynamic\nbodies: `RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()` and `RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(...)` on\n`ctx.physics.world`, then `ctx.physics.bind(body, mesh)` so the mesh follows it.\n\n### Physics toys: platforms, fields, joints, destruction, cloth\n\n```js\nconst lift = ctx.physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased());\nctx.physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.2, 0.15, 1.2), lift);\nctx.physics.addMover(lift, { path: [[0, 0.15, 0], [0, 4, 0]], speed: 1.5, hold: 1 }); // the hero rides it\nctx.physics.addMover(disc, { spin: [0, 0.6, 0] });           // also: swing, curve: orbit(...) / pendulum(...)\nctx.physics.conveyor(beltCollider, [2, 0, 0]);               // carries crates and the hero\nctx.physics.fields.add({ box: [1, 4, 1], at: [4, 4, 0], force: [0, 90, 0], falloff: true, drag: 0.5 }); // an updraft\nctx.physics.explode([0, 0.5, 0], { radius: 4, impulse: 12 });\nhero.launch(16);                                             // a trampoline\nhero.shove = 1.5; hero.weight = 1;                           // push loose bodies; press dynamic floors (both 0 by default)\nropeBridge(ctx, [-3, 0, 0], [3, 0, 0], { planks: 10 });      // also chain, hingeDoor, springPad, seesaw\nconst breaks = new Breakables(ctx);                          // breaks.add / break / crumble; update(dt, hero.groundCollider)\nconst flag = clothGrid({ width: 2, height: 1.2, cols: 12, rows: 8, origin: [0, 3, 0], pin: 'left' });\nctx.scene.add(new SoftMesh(flag, toonMaterial(PALETTE.red)));  // flag.step(dt) per fixed step, mesh.sync() per frame\nconst balls = new InstancedBodies(ctx.physics, ctx.scene, { shape: 'ball', size: 0.2, capacity: 500 }); // one draw call\n```\n\nMovers are functions of their own clock (same at any frame rate). `hero.stats.crushes` counts a\nplatform coming down on the hero: decide what it does. Ledges are fixed bodies only.\n\n### Water, weather, grass, trails and decals\n\n```js\nconst water = new WaterSurface({ size: [14, 8], at: [0, -0.2, 0], waves: WAVES_CALM, ripples: new RippleField({ size: [14, 8], cells: [112, 64] }) });\nconst floaters = new Floaters(ctx.physics.world, (x, z) => (water.covers(x, z) ? water.heightAt(x, z) : null));\napplySky(ctx.engine, skyAt(19));                              // time of day: sun, colours, background\nconst rain = new Precipitation({ kind: 'rain', count: 3000 }); // also 'snow'; rain.intensity, rain.wind\nconst grass = new GrassField({ area: [20, 14], at: [0, 0, 0], count: 6000 });  // grass.push(0, heroFeet, 0.8)\nconst decals = new Decals(ctx.scene);                          // decals.add('scorch', at, normal, { size: 2 })\nctx.scene.add(water, rain, grass, new Trail({ width: 0.3 }));  // trail.push(p, t); trail.update(t, camera)\n```\n\nPer frame: `water.update(t)`, `rain.update(dt, focus)`, `grass.update(t)`, `decals.update(dt)`;\nper fixed step: `floaters.step(dt)` and the ripple field's `step(dt)`.\n\n### Procedural motion and sprites\n\n```js\nconst tail = new SpringChain({ segments: 6, length: 0.12 });   // tail.update(dt, root, [right, up, forward]); tail.points\nconst squash = new Squash();                                   // squash.kick(-5); squash.update(dt); squash.scale() → [xz, y]\nconst legs = new LegStepper({ legs: [{ rest: [-0.6, 0.5], partners: [1] }, { rest: [0.6, 0.5], partners: [0] }] });\nlegs.update(dt, body, yaw, velocity, (x, z) => 0);             // legs.feet[i].at; knee = twoBoneIK(hip, foot, a, b, pole)\nconst sheet = drawSheet({ frame: [16, 16], frames: 4 }, (g, f) => { g.fillStyle = '#ffcd75'; g.fillRect(4, 4 + (f % 2), 8, 8); });\nconst sprites = new SpriteBatch(sheet, { capacity: 100 });     // ctx.scene.add(sprites); sprites.spawn(at, { size: 1 })\n```\n\nSprites face the camera, keep square pixels and are one draw per sheet: `sprites.set(i, { at, frame, flip })`.\n\nA ragdoll from the hero model: `const doll = new Ragdoll(ctx.physics, model, HERO_RAGDOLL); doll.enable({ velocity })`,\nthen `doll.sync()` every frame instead of the mixer; `doll.release({ ground: 0 })` hands it back (blend into `GetUp`).\n\n### Guards, paths, flocks, cars and bullets\n\n```js\nconst nav = NavGrid.fromRows(['#######', '#..c..#', '#.....#', '#######'], { blocked: '#c' });\nconst path = nav.path([-2, 0, -0.5], [2, 0, 0.5]);         // A* (corners only); nav.lineOfSight(ax, az, bx, bz)\nconst flock = new Boids(80, { bounds: { min: [-8, 2, -8], max: [8, 5, 8] } }); // flock.step(dt); flock.pos\nconst car = new Vehicle(ctx.physics, { at: [0, 1, 0] });   // car.drive({ throttle, steer, handbrake }, dt) per fixed step\nconst pool = new BulletPool(3000);                         // emitter({ pattern: 'spiral', every: 0.06 }).update(pool, dt, at, aim)\n```\n\n### Mouse picking and rewind\n\n```js\nctx.camera.rayAt(ctx.engine.input.pointer.x, ctx.engine.input.pointer.y, origin, dir); // then ctx.physics.castRay(origin, dir, 1000)\nconst rewind = new Rewind(ctx.physics, { seconds: 5 }); // rewind.trackAll(); per step: rewind.rewinding = input.isDown('KeyR')\n```\n\n### Procedural generation\n\n```js\nconst noise = createNoise(7); // noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05) about -1..1; noise.ridged2 for crests\nconst land = new Terrain({ size: [40, 30] }).generate((x, z) => 4 * noise.fbm2(x * 0.05, z * 0.05)); // land.erode(); land.attach(ctx.physics); ctx.scene.add(land.mesh)\nconst wfc = new Wfc(patternTiles([{ pattern: ['#.#', '#.#', '#.#'] }, { pattern: ['###', '###', '###'] }]), 10, 8, { seed: 3 }); // wfc.run(); wfc.result\nconst plant = turtle(expand(PLANTS.fern.system, 5), PLANTS.fern.turtle); // plant.branches: { from, to, radius }, plant.leaves\n```\n\n### GPU compute, monitors and mirrors\n\n```js\nconst swarm = new GpuSwarm({ count: 32768 }); ctx.scene.add(swarm.mesh); // per frame: swarm.update(ctx.engine.renderer, dt)\nconst cam = new RenderView({ size: [160, 90] }); const stopCam = cam.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene); // screen.material = cam.screenMaterial(); RenderView.screen(screen)\nconst mirror = new Mirror({ size: [6, 3] }); ctx.scene.add(mirror.mesh); const stopMirror = mirror.attach(ctx.engine.renderer, ctx.scene);\n// in dispose(): stopCam(); stopMirror(); cam.dispose(); mirror.dispose(); swarm.dispose() (a level unload also drops the listeners)\nconst holo = hologramMaterial({ color: PALETTE.cyan }); // forceFieldMaterial, lavaMaterial, marbleMaterial, woodMaterial, crystalMaterial\n```\n\n### Behaviour trees, cutscenes, sound in space\n\n```js\nconst tree = new BehaviorTree(bt.selector('npc', bt.sequence('flee', bt.condition('near?', (b) => b.near), bt.action('run', run)), bt.action('wander', wander)), board); // tree.tick(dt) per step\nconst scene = new Timeline({ shots: [{ at: 0, position: [0, 6, 10], target: [0, 1, 0] }, { at: 4, position: [-1, 2, 4], target: [0, 1, 0] }], cues: [{ at: 1, run: openDoor }], lines: [{ at: 0.5, until: 3, text: 'HELLO.' }] }); // scene.play(); scene.update(dt); scene.camera(); scene.skip()\nconst hum = ctx.audio.loop('coin', { volume: 0 }); // per frame: const s = spatialize({ position: ears, right }, at, { max: 20 }); hum.set({ volume: s.gain, pan: s.pan, muffle: blocked ? 0.85 : 0 })\n```\n\n### Camera\n\n| Preset | View | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `iso` | orthographic 3/4 view, 32° pitch, 45° yaw | the default; `pitch`, `yaw` configurable |\n| `topdown` | straight down, screen up = −Z | |\n| `side` | straight side-on, screen right = +X | `ctx.camera.lockDepth` is true: pass it to the hero |\n| `third` | perspective orbit behind the player | drag or Q/E to orbit |\n| `first` | first person, mouse look (click locks the pointer) | `ctx.camera.hidesTarget`: hide your model |\n| `free`, `fixed` | authoring fly-cam; a frozen view | `{ preset: 'fixed', position, target, fov }` |\n\nConfig: `{ preset, zoom, minZoom, maxZoom, viewHeight, pitch, yaw, fov, distance, stiffness }`.\nPlayers zoom with the wheel or + / -. `engine.setCamera(config, { syncUrl: false })` swaps\nthe preset during play (a cutscene, a minigame); the game's `onCameraChange` fixes up\nanything preset-specific.\n\n### HUD\n\nA pixel canvas over the game, in art pixels, drawn after the filters (always crisp).\nIt is retained: `clear()` then redraw each frame.\n\n```js\nctx.hud.clear();\nctx.hud.text(6, 6, `SCORE ${score}`, { color: 'sand' });            // 5×7 font, UPPERCASE\nctx.hud.text(6, 6, 'LIVES 3', { anchor: 'top-right' });              // anchors: top-left (default), top,\nctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });        //   top-right, left, center, right, bottom-*\nctx.hud.rect(0, 0, ctx.hud.width, 12, 'ink', 'bottom-left');\nctx.hud.sprite(6, 20, ['.oo.', 'oyyo', '.oo.'], { o: 'orange', y: 'sand' }); // '.' is transparent\n```\n\n### Audio\n\n```js\nctx.audio.play('coin');                       // built-in: jump doubleJump land coin step skid punch groundPound whoosh hurt fanfare\nctx.audio.play('coin', { pitch: 5, volume: 0.6, pan: -0.3 });\nctx.audio.register('zap', { wave: 'square', duty: 0.25, freq: 1200, freqEnd: 200, decay: 0.15, volume: 0.3 });\nctx.audio.playMusic(SONG);                    // a Song (data, see the top-down recipe); playMusic(null) stops\nawait ctx.audio.load('door', 'assets/door.ogg');\n```\n\nSounds are synthesised from data, so a game needs no audio files. Browsers start sound on\nthe first key or tap; before that, calls are silent (and still counted in `ctx.audio.counts`).\n\n### Particles and lights\n\n```js\nctx.particles.burst('dust', at);              // built-in: dust skid sparkle smoke impact\nctx.particles.burst('sparkle', at, { count: 20, colors: ['white', 'cyan'] });\nctx.particles.register('ember', { count: [3, 5], life: [0.6, 1], speed: [1, 2], gravity: -2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['sand', 'orange', 'red'] });\n\nconst torch = ctx.lights.request({ position: [3, 1.6, 4], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 6, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\nctx.lights.request({ follow: heroModel, offset: [0, 1.4, 0], intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 });\ntorch.release();\nctx.engine.sun.intensity = 0.6;               // the sun and ambient light are the engine's: dim them for night\n```\n\nParticle sizes are whole art pixels and colours are palette names. Point lights come from a\nfixed pool (4 to 16 by quality), handed to the most important requests: never add your own\n`PointLight`s (each one recompiles every material).\n\n### Game feel and scene changes\n\n```js\nctx.engine.hitstop(0.08);                    // freeze the game 0.08 s when a hit lands (rendering goes on)\nctx.engine.shake.add(0.4);                   // screen shake: trauma 0..1 that drains; whole art pixels\nctx.engine.timeScale = 0.25;                 // slow motion (1 normal, 0 a frozen menu); loadGame resets it\nctx.engine.screen.flash('white', { duration: 0.1 });\nctx.engine.screen.shockwave(blastAt, { radius: 0.4 });        // a ring that pushes the picture outward\nawait ctx.engine.screen.cover('iris', { center: heroAt });    // fade iris diamonds dissolve dither blinds wipe curtain mosaic\nawait ctx.engine.loadGame(new Level2());\nawait ctx.engine.screen.reveal('iris');\nctx.tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' }); // game time, cleared on unload\nctx.tweens.call(1.5, () => spawnWave());     // a timer\nctx.engine.setSunDirection([1, 0.3, 0.2]);   // a low dawn sun; the next level resets it\n```\n\nTransitions, flashes and shockwaves are uniforms at the end of the pipeline: they never\ncompile a shader, work with every look, and keep running while a level loads.\n\n### Looks\n\nA `Look` sets the art style per layer: `{ scene, actors, environment }`. Each layer is\n`{ pixel: { size, outline, crease } | null, filters: [...] }` (`null` renders it clean, at\nfull resolution); `scene` filters apply to the whole frame on top.\n\n```js\nengine.setLook(LOOK_PRESETS.noir);            // or Engine.start(game, { look }) / ?look=noir\nengine.setLook({\n  scene: [{ id: 'vignette' }],\n  actors: { pixel: { size: 2, outline: 0.8, crease: 0.1 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4 } }] },\n});\n```\n\nNamed looks (`LOOK_PRESETS`): `none` (the default pixel art) `no_filters` `eight_bit`\n`sixteen_bit` `playstation` `arcade` `handheld` `famicom` `home_computer` `vhs_rental`\n`spectrum` `mac_classic` `pico` `dream` `spooky` `pixel_heroes` `pixel_world` `chunky_world`\n`cel_cartoon` `cel_heroes` `retro_heroes` `spotlight` `sketchbook` `dream_world`\n`handheld_heroes` `noir` `sin_city` `comic_book` `pop_art` `storybook` `neon_nights`\n`heat_vision` `night_ops` `ps1_horror` `found_footage` `old_photo` `virtual_boy` `pico_world`.\nFilters (`FILTERS`, each with parameters and presets, see API.md): `cel 8bit 16bit ps1\nsweetie16 pico8 nes c64 zx ega cga gameboy gbpocket virtualboy onebit dither posterize adjust\ngrayscale sepia invert bleach sunset moonlight thermal nightvision scanlines lcd crt vignette\nchromatic grain vhs ntsc bloom halftone sketch`. Changing parameters or pixel sizes is free;\nchanging which filters are on rebuilds the pipeline once.\n\n## 5. Recipes\n\nEach recipe is a whole game, built into the kit as `examples/<name>.html`. Steal from them.\n\n### Side-scroller: levels as data, enemies, level progression\n\n```js side-scroller\n// Cliff Run: a side-scroller. Run (A/D or arrows), jump (Space; again on landing for a higher\n// one), stomp the slimes, reach the flag. Two levels, then it starts over.\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PALETTE, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, CylinderGeometry, Group, Mesh, SphereGeometry, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n// Levels are data. platforms: [left x, right x, top y]; slimes: [x, patrol half-width]; flag: x.\nconst LEVELS = [\n  { platforms: [[-4, 10, 0], [12, 15, 1.2], [17, 20, 2.2], [22, 32, 0], [34, 37, 1.4], [39, 48, 0]], slimes: [[27, 3], [43, 2]], flag: 46 },\n  { platforms: [[-4, 6, 0], [8, 10, 1.4], [12, 14, 2.6], [16, 18, 1.4], [20, 28, 0], [30, 31.5, 1.2], [33.5, 35, 2.2], [37, 48, 0]], slimes: [[24, 3], [41, 2]], flag: 46 },\n];\nconst START = [-2, 0, 0];\n\nclass CliffRun {\n  name = 'Cliff Run';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/tree.glb'];\n  slimes = [];\n  stomps = 0;\n  falls = 0;\n  clearedAt = null;\n  leaving = false;\n  startTime = null;\n  feet = new Vector3();\n  from = new Vector3();\n  target = new Vector3();\n\n  constructor(level = 0) {\n    this.level = level;\n    this.data = LEVELS[level];\n  }\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n    const parts = [];\n    for (const [x0, x1, top] of this.data.platforms) {\n      const w = x1 - x0;\n      const h = top + 4; // every platform reaches down out of view\n      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [(x0 + x1) / 2, top - h / 2, 0], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, 2] });\n      const body = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, 4), toonMaterial(palette.sand));\n      body.position.set((x0 + x1) / 2, top - h / 2, 0);\n      const grass = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w + 0.1, 0.3, 4.1), toonMaterial(palette.green));\n      grass.position.set((x0 + x1) / 2, top - 0.14, 0);\n      for (const m of [body, grass]) m.receiveShadow = m.castShadow = true;\n      parts.push(body, grass);\n    }\n    // The flag: no collider, a trigger ends the level.\n    const pole = new Mesh(new CylinderGeometry(0.06, 0.06, 3, 6), toonMaterial(palette.white));\n    pole.position.set(this.data.flag, 1.5, -1);\n    const cloth = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.9, 0.6, 0.06), toonMaterial(palette.red));\n    cloth.position.set(this.data.flag + 0.45, 2.65, -1);\n    parts.push(pole, cloth);\n    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n    physics.trigger({ box: [0.5, 2, 2] }, [this.data.flag, 2, 0], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.clear(ctx) });\n\n    // Scenery behind the lane: trees (a built-in model) and clouds.\n    const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb');\n    for (const x of [2, 25, 41]) {\n      const t = tree.scene.clone(true);\n      t.position.set(x, 0, -1.4);\n      scene.add(t);\n    }\n    for (const [x, y] of [[0, 7], [14, 8], [30, 6.5], [44, 7.5]]) {\n      const cloud = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(3, 0.8, 0.8), toonMaterial(palette.white));\n      cloud.position.set(x, y, -6);\n      scene.add(cloud);\n    }\n\n    // Slimes: a squashed sphere with eyes, patrolling; stomp them from above.\n    for (const [x, range] of this.data.slimes) {\n      const mesh = new Group();\n      const blob = new Mesh(new SphereGeometry(0.45, 12, 8), toonMaterial(palette.lime));\n      blob.scale.y = 0.7;\n      blob.position.y = 0.3;\n      blob.castShadow = true;\n      mesh.add(blob);\n      for (const ex of [-0.15, 0.15]) {\n        const eye = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.1, 0.14, 0.05), toonMaterial(palette.ink));\n        eye.position.set(ex, 0.42, 0.4);\n        mesh.add(eye);\n      }\n      mesh.position.set(x, 0, 0);\n      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors');\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      this.slimes.push({ mesh, x, range, phase: x, dead: false });\n    }\n\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: START, lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n  }\n\n  clear(ctx) {\n    this.clearedAt = ctx.time;\n    this.hero.celebrate();\n    ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', [this.data.flag, 3, -1], { count: 30 });\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n    if (this.clearedAt !== null) this.input.move.set(0, 0, 0); // level clear: stand still and celebrate\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n    const f = this.hero.feetInto(this.feet);\n    for (const s of this.slimes) {\n      if (s.dead) continue;\n      s.phase += dt * 1.3;\n      s.mesh.position.x = s.x + Math.sin(s.phase) * s.range;\n      const dx = f.x - s.mesh.position.x;\n      if (Math.abs(dx) > 0.7 || f.y > 0.9 || f.y < -0.3) continue;\n      if (this.hero.vy < 0 && f.y > 0.3) {\n        // landed on it: stomp and bounce\n        s.dead = true;\n        s.mesh.visible = false;\n        this.stomps++;\n        ctx.audio.play('punch');\n        ctx.particles.burst('impact', s.mesh.position);\n        this.hero.jump('Jump');\n      } else if (this.hero.hurt(this.from.set(-dx, 0, 0))) {\n        // `from` points from the hero to what hit it (slime - hero): knocked back the other way\n        ctx.audio.play('hurt');\n      }\n    }\n    if (f.y < -8) {\n      this.falls++;\n      ctx.audio.play('hurt');\n      this.hero.teleport(START);\n    }\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.startTime ??= ctx.time;\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n    const feet = this.model.position;\n    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.target.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n    this.shadow.visible = !!ground;\n    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n    if (this.clearedAt !== null && ctx.time - this.clearedAt > 2.5 && !this.leaving) {\n      this.leaving = true;\n      void ctx.engine.loadGame(new CliffRun((this.level + 1) % LEVELS.length));\n    }\n    const time = (this.clearedAt ?? ctx.time) - this.startTime;\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LEVEL ${this.level + 1}`, { color: 'sand' });\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 16, `TIME ${time.toFixed(1)}  STOMPS ${this.stomps}`);\n    if (this.clearedAt !== null) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'LEVEL CLEAR!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.feet.set(...START);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 1.2, p.z);\n  }\n\n  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth);\n    this.model.visible = !ctx.camera.hidesTarget;\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return `level ${this.level + 1}${this.clearedAt !== null ? ' clear' : ''}, stomps ${this.stomps}, falls ${this.falls}`;\n  }\n}\n\nawait Engine.start(new CliffRun(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 }, background: PALETTE.sky }));\n```\n\n### Top-down at night: lights, pushable crates, music, custom particles\n\n```js top-down\n// Lantern Night: walk (WASD / arrows), push both crates onto the glowing pads (walk into a\n// crate to push it), then leave through the gate in the north wall.\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, LOOK_PRESETS, mergeStaticMeshes, PALETTE, PlatformerCharacter, RAPIER, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Color, CylinderGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\nconst SONG = {\n  bpm: 84,\n  tracks: { lead: { wave: 'triangle', decay: 0.3, volume: 0.12 }, bass: { wave: 'square', duty: 0.125, decay: 0.25, volume: 0.05 } },\n  patterns: { a: { lead: 'A4 . C5 . E5 . . . D5 . C5 . B4 . . .', bass: 'A2 . . . . . . . F2 . . . . . . .' } },\n  order: ['a'],\n};\nconst PADS = [[-3, -4], [3, -4]];\nconst CRATES = [[-3, 1], [3, 1]];\n\nclass LanternNight {\n  name = 'Lantern Night';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/tree.glb'];\n  crates = [];\n  pads = [];\n  solved = false;\n  escaped = false;\n  nextFirefly = 0;\n  target = new Vector3();\n  at = new Vector3();\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette, engine } = ctx;\n    this.sparkle = ctx.random.fork('fireflies'); // drawn per frame (cosmetic): its own stream, so gameplay's never depends on the frame rate\n    // Night: dim the engine's sun and ambient light (reset for the next game on unload).\n    engine.sun.color.setHex(PALETTE.sky);\n    engine.sun.intensity = 1.2;\n    engine.ambient.color.setHex(PALETTE.navy);\n    engine.ambient.intensity = 1.4;\n    scene.background = new Color(PALETTE.ink);\n\n    const parts = [];\n    const wall = (x, z, w, d) => {\n      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, 0.6, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, 0.6, d / 2] });\n      const m = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, 1.2, d), toonMaterial(palette.slate));\n      m.position.set(x, 0.6, z);\n      m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n      parts.push(m);\n    };\n    const floor = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(30, 1, 30), toonMaterial(palette.teal));\n    floor.position.y = -0.5;\n    floor.receiveShadow = true;\n    parts.push(floor);\n    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, -0.5, 0], halfExtents: [15, 0.5, 15] });\n    wall(-4.75, -8, 6.5, 1); // north wall, with a 3 m gap for the gate\n    wall(4.75, -8, 6.5, 1);\n    wall(0, 8, 17, 1);\n    wall(-8, 0, 1, 15);\n    wall(8, 0, 1, 15);\n    for (const [x, z] of [[-6.5, -6.5], [6.5, -6.5], [-6.5, 6.5], [6.5, 6.5]]) {\n      const post = new Mesh(new CylinderGeometry(0.12, 0.15, 1.6, 6), toonMaterial(palette.plum));\n      post.position.set(x, 0.8, z);\n      parts.push(post);\n      ctx.lights.request({ position: [x, 1.8, z], color: 0xffa040, intensity: 7, radius: 7, flicker: 'torch' });\n    }\n    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n\n    // The gate: a collider we remove when the puzzle is solved.\n    this.gate = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(3, 1.6, 0.6), toonMaterial(palette.red));\n    this.gate.position.set(0, 0.8, -8);\n    scene.add(this.gate);\n    this.gateCollider = physics.addStaticBox({ position: [0, 0.8, -8], halfExtents: [1.5, 0.8, 0.3] });\n    physics.trigger({ box: [1.5, 1, 0.5] }, [0, 1, -9.2], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.escape(ctx) });\n\n    // Pads and crates. A crate is a dynamic Rapier body the hero pushes (tag 'pushable').\n    for (const [x, z] of PADS) {\n      const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1.2, 0.06, 1.2), toonMaterial(palette.navy));\n      mesh.position.set(x, 0.03, z);\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      this.pads.push({ x, z, mesh, lit: false });\n    }\n    for (const [x, z] of CRATES) {\n      const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(0.98, 0.98, 0.98), toonMaterial(palette.orange));\n      mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors');\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      const body = physics.world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic().setTranslation(x, 0.5, z).setLinearDamping(4).enabledRotations(false, false, false));\n      physics.tag(physics.world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(0.49, 0.49, 0.49).setFriction(0.1).setDensity(8), body), 'pushable');\n      physics.bind(body, mesh);\n      this.crates.push(body);\n    }\n\n    const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb');\n    for (let i = 0; i < 14; i++) {\n      const a = (i / 14) * Math.PI * 2;\n      const t = tree.scene.clone(true);\n      t.position.set(Math.cos(a) * 11.5, 0, Math.sin(a) * 11.5);\n      scene.add(t);\n    }\n\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 5], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n    ctx.lights.request({ follow: this.model, offset: [0, 1.5, 0], color: 0xffe0a0, intensity: 5, radius: 6, priority: 8 }); // the lantern\n\n    ctx.particles.register('firefly', { count: [1, 1], life: [1.5, 2.5], speed: [0.2, 0.5], gravity: -0.2, size: [1, 1], colors: ['lime', 'sand', 'lime'] });\n    ctx.audio.register('chime', { wave: 'triangle', freq: 880, decay: 0.4, volume: 0.3, arp: [0, 4, 7], arpRate: 0.06 });\n    ctx.audio.playMusic(SONG, 'night');\n  }\n\n  escape(ctx) {\n    this.escaped = true;\n    this.hero.celebrate();\n    ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n    if (this.escaped) this.input.move.set(0, 0, 0); // the end: stand and celebrate\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n    const feet = this.model.position;\n    this.shadow.place(feet.x, 0, feet.z, feet.y); // flat floor: the ground is y = 0\n    // A pad lights when a crate sits on it; both lit opens the gate.\n    for (const pad of this.pads) {\n      const on = this.crates.some((b) => Math.hypot(b.translation().x - pad.x, b.translation().z - pad.z) < 0.5);\n      if (on && !pad.lit) {\n        pad.lit = true;\n        pad.mesh.material = toonMaterial(ctx.palette.lime);\n        ctx.lights.request({ position: [pad.x, 0.6, pad.z], color: 0x9cf060, intensity: 5, radius: 4, flicker: 'pulse' });\n        ctx.audio.play('chime');\n      }\n    }\n    if (!this.solved && this.pads.every((p) => p.lit)) {\n      this.solved = true;\n      ctx.physics.remove(this.gateCollider);\n      this.gate.visible = false;\n      ctx.particles.burst('smoke', this.gate.position, { count: 24 });\n      ctx.audio.play('groundPound');\n    }\n    if (ctx.time > this.nextFirefly) {\n      this.nextFirefly = ctx.time + 0.3;\n      ctx.particles.burst('firefly', this.at.set(this.sparkle.range(-7, 7), this.sparkle.range(0.5, 2), this.sparkle.range(-7, 7)));\n    }\n    const lit = this.pads.filter((p) => p.lit).length;\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, this.escaped ? 'YOU ESCAPED!' : this.solved ? 'THE GATE IS OPEN' : `PADS ${lit}/${this.pads.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 5);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return this.escaped ? 'escaped' : `pads ${this.pads.filter((p) => p.lit).length}/${this.pads.length}`;\n  }\n}\n\nawait Engine.start(new LanternNight(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'topdown', zoom: 1.1 }, look: LOOK_PRESETS.dream }));\n```\n\n### Looks: mixing art styles per layer\n\n```js looks\n// Look Lab: one scene, six looks. Keys 1-6 pick a look, T toggles chunky pixels on the custom\n// one. Walk around (WASD): characters & objects (the 'actors' layer) and the environment each\n// get their own look.\nimport { compileClips, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, LOOK_PRESETS, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n// A look of our own: pixel-art characters on a clean, cel-shaded world, a vignette on top.\nconst STORYBOOK_HEROES = {\n  scene: [{ id: 'vignette', params: { intensity: 0.35 } }],\n  actors: { pixel: { size: 1, outline: 0.85, crease: 0.2 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4, ink: 0.6 } }] },\n};\nconst LOOKS = [\n  ['default', LOOK_PRESETS.none],\n  ['storybook heroes', STORYBOOK_HEROES],\n  ['pixel heroes', LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes],\n  ['noir', LOOK_PRESETS.noir],\n  ['sin city', LOOK_PRESETS.sin_city],\n  ['handheld', LOOK_PRESETS.handheld],\n];\n\nclass LookLab {\n  name = 'Look Lab';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb', 'assets/tree.glb'];\n  current = 1;\n  target = new Vector3();\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n    const parts = [];\n    for (const [x, z, w, h, d, c] of [[0, 0, 16, 1, 12, 'green'], [-4, -3, 2, 2, 2, 'slate'], [4, -3, 3, 1, 2, 'sand'], [0, -5, 6, 3, 1, 'plum']]) {\n      const y = c === 'green' ? -0.5 : h / 2;\n      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n      const m = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(palette[c]));\n      m.position.set(x, y, z);\n      m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n      parts.push(m);\n    }\n    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n    for (const [x, z] of [[-6, 3], [6, 2], [-2, -4]]) {\n      const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb'); // environment: no layer tag\n      tree.scene.position.set(x, 0, z);\n      scene.add(tree.scene);\n    }\n    // Objects on the actors layer: coins and a crate.\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb');\n    this.coins = [[-1.5, 0.6, 1], [0, 0.6, 1], [1.5, 0.6, 1]].map(([x, y, z]) => {\n      const c = coin.scene.clone(true);\n      c.position.set(x, y, z);\n      setLookLayer(c, 'actors');\n      scene.add(c);\n      return c;\n    });\n    const crate = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), toonMaterial(palette.orange));\n    crate.position.set(3, 0.5, 2);\n    crate.castShadow = true;\n    setLookLayer(crate, 'actors');\n    scene.add(crate);\n    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [3, 0.5, 2], halfExtents: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] });\n\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL);\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    scene.add(this.model);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 3], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n    for (const c of this.coins) c.rotation.y += dt * 2;\n    for (let i = 0; i < LOOKS.length; i++) {\n      if (ctx.input.wasPressed(`Digit${i + 1}`)) {\n        this.current = i;\n        ctx.engine.setLook(LOOKS[i][1]);\n      }\n    }\n    if (ctx.input.wasPressed('KeyT')) {\n      // Parameters and pixel sizes are uniforms: changing them never rebuilds the pipeline.\n      STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size = STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size === 1 ? 3 : 1;\n      if (this.current === 1) ctx.engine.setLook(STORYBOOK_HEROES);\n    }\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LOOK ${this.current + 1}/${LOOKS.length}: ${LOOKS[this.current][0].toUpperCase()}`, { color: 'sand' });\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, '1-6 LOOKS  T CHUNKY', { anchor: 'bottom-left' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 3);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return `look ${LOOKS[this.current][0]}`;\n  }\n}\n\nawait Engine.start(new LookLab(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.3 }, look: STORYBOOK_HEROES }));\n```\n\n## 6. Rules\n\n1. **Import everything from `./pixel-engine.js`.** three.js is `THREE` (`three/webgpu`) and\n   `TSL`; Rapier is `RAPIER`. Never load three.js or Rapier from a CDN or npm as well: two\n   copies break `instanceof` checks and the renderer.\n2. **Never edit `pixel-engine.js`.** Upgrade by replacing it (section 10).\n3. **No WebGL-era three.js APIs.** The renderer is `WebGPURenderer`: `WebGLRenderer`,\n   `EffectComposer`, `ShaderMaterial`, `onBeforeCompile` and GLSL don't work. Custom shading is\n   TSL on node materials; post effects are looks.\n4. **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** Move characters and apply forces at\n   the fixed 60 Hz step; animate, spin pickups and draw the HUD per frame. Use `ctx.time`\n   (it stops while paused) and the `dt` you are given, never `performance.now()`. Anything\n   random comes from `ctx.random` (`range`, `int`, `chance`, `pick`, `fork`), never\n   `Math.random`: the same seed (`?seed=`) and input then replay the same game\n   (`engine.recording()`, `engine.replay()`, section 8). Draw it in `setup`, `fixedUpdate` and\n   event handlers; randomness drawn per frame in `update` (cosmetic) comes from a fork made in\n   `setup`, so the frame rate never changes what gameplay draws. Read buttons from `ctx.input`\n   (mouse buttons are keys: `isDown('MouseLeft')`; a finger is `Touch`), and data from the page (a save in storage, a\n   text field) through `ctx.input.send(data)` → `ctx.input.commands` next frame, so they replay too.\n5. **Physics owns positions.** Don't set `mesh.position` on things with bodies: `physics.bind`\n   them, or let the character controller pose its model. Static blocks: one collider each,\n   the same size as the mesh.\n6. **Palette colours and toon materials** (`toonMaterial(palette.x)`), chunky shapes (≥ 0.25 m).\n   Other materials render, but break the look.\n7. **Lights come from `ctx.lights`**, never `new PointLight()`. Dim `ctx.engine.sun` and\n   `ctx.engine.ambient` for night scenes.\n8. **Tag characters and objects** with `setLookLayer(root, 'actors')`, so looks can treat\n   them apart from the environment.\n9. **Reuse vectors in per-frame code** (`hero.feetInto(v)`, a `target` vector for\n   `cameraTarget`): no garbage per frame.\n10. **Give `status()` a meaningful line** (score, lives, level, state): the check tool and\n   other agents read it.\n11. **Clean up what the engine doesn't own**: DOM you add, timers and listeners go in `dispose(ctx)`.\n\n## 7. Common mistakes\n\n| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Blank page, console: CORS / \"Failed to load module script\" | opened with `file://` | serve the folder over HTTP |\n| Console: \"Multiple instances of Three.js being imported\" | three.js loaded twice | use `THREE` from the bundle, nothing else |\n| A red **GAME ERROR** box | an exception in a hook | the box and `engine.errors` have the message, the console the stack: fix the first one |\n| \"Failed to start: ...\" | `setup()` threw (often a bad model path or a typo'd name) | read the message; check the path or the name in API.md |\n| The hero falls through the world | no collider under the mesh | `physics.addStaticBox` for every solid block |\n| The hero is stuck at the start | spawned inside a block | `position` is the feet: put it on the top surface |\n| A trigger never fires | wrong tag, or it doesn't overlap the body | `tag: 'character'` only matches the hero; level geometry needs `includeStatic: true` |\n| A colour is black or a material missing | a colour name that isn't in the palette | the 16 names in section 4 |\n| A model 404s | path not relative to the page | `assets/x.glb` next to `index.html`; built-ins need no file |\n| HUD text shows gaps | characters the 5×7 font doesn't have | uppercase letters, digits, common punctuation |\n| A key does nothing in `fixedUpdate` | `wasPressed` there | `consumePress` in `fixedUpdate`, `wasPressed` in `update` |\n| The ⚙ sits over the HUD's top-right corner on a phone | the settings button (on a mouse it shows only while the pointer moves) | `settings: { button: 'top-left' }`, or `false` and `engine.settings.open()` from your own menu |\n| Side-scroller hero drifts off the lane | `lockDepth` not passed | `lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth` |\n| The camera doesn't follow | `cameraTarget` returns nothing before `setup` ends | return a fallback vector (see the quick start) |\n| Slow, low fps | hundreds of separate meshes | `mergeStaticMeshes` the static level; share materials (`toonMaterial` caches) |\n| Everything is dark | a dimmed sun with no lights | `ctx.lights.request(...)` torches, or raise `ctx.engine.ambient.intensity` |\n| No sound | the browser waits for a key or tap | expected: sound starts on the first input |\n\n## 8. Debugging and checking\n\nThe engine is on `window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__` once it runs (also the value `Engine.start` returns).\nA tool waits on `window.__PIXEL_READY__` (it resolves with the engine, or rejects with why the start\nfailed); a game puts its own page handles in `ready(ctx)`, which runs then and after each `loadGame`.\n\n```js\nconst e = await window.__PIXEL_READY__;   // step() before this throws\ne.state();                // version, backend, fps, camera, look, game status, errors, gpuErrors, ...\ne.errors;                 // exceptions thrown by the game's hooks in the render loop (also on screen)\ne.step(60);               // manual time: exactly 60 frames (1 s), a hook's exception thrown to you; e.manual = false resumes\n                          // step() draws nothing, so a page screenshot still shows the old frame: read the new one with\n                          // await e.renderer.capture() (pixels, no HUD), or set e.manual = false and let a frame draw\ne.input.setKey('KeyD', true); e.step(30); e.input.setKey('KeyD', false);  // play by script\nconst rec = e.recording(); const end = e.fingerprint();            // this level's input as JSON, a hash of its state\nawait e.replay(rec, () => e.loadGame(new MyGame())); e.fingerprint() === end;  // replayed: the same game\n// Engine.headless(game, { assets }): the same game in Node (below)\nconst snap = await e.snapshot(); e.step(300); await e.restore(snap);   // a save-state (Game.snapshot / restore): back at once\nawait e.renderer.capture();   // { width, height, pixels }: the exact frame (without the HUD canvas)\ne.game;                   // your game object: e.game.hero.teleport([x, y, z]) to test a spot\ne.setLook(e.lookPresets.noir); e.setCamera({ preset: 'third' }, { syncUrl: false });\ne.audio.counts; e.particles.alive; e.physics.counts(); e.hud.canvas;\n```\n\nURL flags (with `withUrlOptions` in `Engine.start`): `?debug=1` (debug panel),\n`?look=noir`, `?filters=crt,scanlines`,\n`?camera=third`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?mode=raw` (no pixel pass), `?res=320`, `?touch=1`,\n`?quality=low`, `?fps=30`, `?seed=7` (`ctx.random`); they win over the player's saved settings\n(`e.settings.values`). Hotkeys, with `?debug=1`: P pixel/raw, R resolution, \\` debug panel, [ and ]\ncycle looks, M mute.\n\n**`check.mjs` plays a page in a real browser** and fails on anything wrong:\n\n```\nnode check.mjs                                  # index.html: start, run 90 frames, screenshot\nnode check.mjs --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyD*40   # hold each key for that many frames, in order\nnode check.mjs examples/side-scroller.html --cameras --looks noir,handheld\nnode check.mjs \"index.html?look=handheld&zoom=1.5\"   # with URL flags\nnode check.mjs --sheets models.js               # sheets of your own models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n```\n\nIt plays on WebGPU in Chromium's headless shell, so it needs no display. It writes\n`check/<page>.png` (the frame with the HUD), the camera and look shots and\n`check/report.json` (errors, warnings, `state()`), and prints a summary: the backend, frames\nand your `status()`. Exit code 0: the game started, ran with no page, console, game or GPU\nerrors and drew a real picture; 1: it didn't; 2: the tool couldn't run (a browser without\nWebGPU, bad arguments, no page, no browser; `--help` lists the options). It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and Playwright\n(`npm i -D playwright && npx playwright install chromium`; a global install works too), or\n`playwright-core` plus a Chromium in `CHROMIUM_PATH`. **Look at the PNGs**: a passing check only proves nothing crashed.\n\n**Play it in Node.** `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` runs a game as `Engine.start` does, minus\nthe GPU and DOM, on manual time, from the kit alone; `assets` reads the game's files.\n`node check.mjs --headless game.js` plays its `createGame()` (the starter's) for the `--keys`,\nreplays it in a fresh game and fails on an error or a replay that ends elsewhere. By hand:\n\n```js\n// test.mjs, beside game.js\nimport { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport { Engine } from './pixel-engine.js';\nimport { createGame } from './game.js';\n\nconst assets = async (path) => new Uint8Array(await readFile(new URL(path, import.meta.url))).buffer;\nconst engine = await Engine.headless(createGame(), { assets });\nengine.step(60); // input.setKey to play; a hook's error throws\nconst end = engine.fingerprint();\nawait engine.replay(engine.recording(), () => engine.loadGame(createGame()));\nconsole.log(engine.state().status, engine.fingerprint() === end);\n```\n\nCode that touches `document` guards it, as the starter does.\n\n## 9. Before you hand back\n\n- [ ] `node check.mjs` exits 0 for every page, with `--keys` that actually play the game\n      (reach a goal, collect something, lose a life).\n- [ ] `node check.mjs --headless game.js` exits 0 with the same `--keys`.\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots: the scene reads, the HUD is legible, nothing is black or\n      clipped, the hero is on the ground.\n- [ ] `status()` reports the game's state, and it changes as you play.\n- [ ] No WebGL-era APIs, no second three.js, no `new PointLight`, no unknown palette names.\n- [ ] The controls are written down (the header comment of `game.js`, or on screen).\n- [ ] On a phone (or `?touch=1`) the game is playable with the touch buttons, or you set\n      `touchButtons` for its own actions.\n\n## 10. Versions\n\n`ENGINE_VERSION` (also `Engine.version` and `state().version`) is the engine's version, and\nthe kit's `CHANGELOG.md` says what changed in each. To upgrade a game, replace\n`pixel-engine.js` (and `pixel-engine.d.ts`, `types/` if you use them), read the changelog\nentries since the old version, then run `node check.mjs` again. The newest release of the kit is\nat the URL in `manifest.json`'s `latest`, and every release (from 0.39.0, its changelog as the\nnotes) stays downloadable from `releases`. The kit's `README.md` says how to build\nit from the engine's repository (`source`) instead.\n","numLines":1116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1116}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0158NkGbiS4Vg22n635n7VwK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94RQTM1JF4RLN2WZZ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GqW3K8QyxpAz2rUqzmyFSf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1389,"cache_read_input_tokens":31410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1389},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"178d1192-8768-4c44-9d3d-a40e1bb608c3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:34.665Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94R9px9QSWHiu5Ucd1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GqW3K8QyxpAz2rUqzmyFSf":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GqW3K8QyxpAz2rUqzmyFSf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n2\t\n3\tAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\n4\tplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\n5\tround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\n6\tscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\n7\tround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n8\t\n9\t**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n10\t\n11\tThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\n12\tdescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n13\t\n14\t- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n15\t- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n16\t\n17\tTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n18\t\n19\t> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n20\t> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n21\t> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## Modes\n26\t\n27\tA game declares one mode.\n28\t\n29\t| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n30\t|---|---|---|\n31\t| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n32\t| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n33\t| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n34\t\n35\t- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n36\t  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n37\t- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n38\t  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n39\t  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n40\t- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n41\t  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n42\t  world the host sends them.\n43\t\n44\tGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n45\t`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## A round\n50\t\n51\t1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n52\t   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n53\t   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n54\t   (*The card*).\n55\t2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n56\t3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n57\t4. The game reports how it goes.\n58\t5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n59\t   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n60\t   over the game, then removes it.\n61\t\n62\t### Tags\n63\t\n64\tIn the game's `<head>`:\n65\t\n66\t```html\n67\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n68\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n69\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n70\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n71\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n75\t  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n76\t  is in that range.\n77\t- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n78\t- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n79\t  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n80\t\n81\tOptional:\n82\t\n83\t- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n84\t  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n85\t- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n86\t  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n87\t\n88\t### The client\n89\t\n90\t[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\n91\tis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\n92\tafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n93\t\n94\t- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n95\t  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n96\t- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n97\t  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n98\t  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n99\t- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n100\t  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n101\t  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n102\t\n103\tThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\n104\tedited copy is not replaced.\n105\t\n106\t### Joining\n107\t\n108\t- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n109\t- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n110\t  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n111\t- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n112\t  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n113\t  the game runs as it does on its own.\n114\t- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n115\t  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n116\t- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n117\t  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n118\t  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n119\t\n120\t`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n121\t\n122\t| Field | |\n123\t|---|---|\n124\t| `me` | this copy's player id |\n125\t| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n126\t| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n127\t| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n128\t| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n129\t| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n130\t\n131\tNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\n132\tfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n133\t\n134\t### The card\n135\t\n136\tBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\n137\tnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\n138\tthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n139\t\n140\t```js\n141\tAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\n145\tscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\n146\ttitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\n147\tit deserves.\n148\t\n149\tWhile it is on, the card shows:\n150\t\n151\t- the game's name;\n152\t- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n153\t- how to win the round;\n154\t- the controls this player will use;\n155\t- the time left, from the `card` event.\n156\t\n157\tAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\n158\ton the card:\n159\t\n160\t- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n161\t  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n162\t- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n163\t  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n164\t\n165\tNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\n166\tchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\n167\tnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n168\t`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n169\t\n170\t`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n171\t\n172\t| Field | |\n173\t|---|---|\n174\t| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n175\t| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n176\t| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n177\t| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n178\t| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n179\t| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n180\t\n181\t### The start\n182\t\n183\t`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n184\t`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\n185\tgive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n186\t`start`.\n187\t\n188\t### Reporting\n189\t\n190\t- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n191\t  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n192\t  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n193\t- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n194\t  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n195\t  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n196\t- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n197\t  by the host only:\n198\t  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n199\t    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n200\t    placed last.\n201\t  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n202\t  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n203\t\n204\t### Time-up and the end\n205\t\n206\t- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n207\t  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n208\t  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n209\t  `finish()` by then is void.\n210\t- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n211\t  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n212\t  game, then removes it.\n213\t\n214\t| `end` reason | |\n215\t|---|---|\n216\t| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n217\t| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n218\t| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n219\t| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n220\t| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n221\t\n222\t### Leaving\n223\t\n224\t- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n225\t  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n226\t- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n227\t- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n228\t  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n229\t- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n230\t  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n231\t  that player leaving.\n232\t\n233\t### Scoring\n234\t\n235\t- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n236\t  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n237\t  progress.\n238\t- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n239\t  result instead of medals.\n240\t- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n241\t\n242\t`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\n243\tresults:\n244\t\n245\t- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n246\t  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n247\t- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n248\t  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n249\t\n250\tPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n251\t\n252\t---\n253\t\n254\t## Sending data between copies\n255\t\n256\t### Parallel\n257\t\n258\tThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n259\t\n260\t### Ghost\n261\t\n262\tEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\n263\tthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\n264\tonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\n265\tcharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n266\t\n267\t### Shared world\n268\t\n269\t- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n270\t  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n271\t- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n272\t  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n273\t  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n274\t- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n275\t  every copy.\n276\t\n277\t### `send()` and `message`\n278\t\n279\t- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n280\t  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n281\t- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n282\t  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n283\t  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n284\t- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n285\t- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n286\t  client drops the rest.\n287\t- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n288\t  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n289\t  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n290\t  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n291\t\n292\t---\n293\t\n294\t## Rules\n295\t\n296\tIn a session:\n297\t\n298\t- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n299\t  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n300\t- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n301\t  names. Names come from the hub.\n302\t- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n303\t  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n304\t  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n305\t- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n306\t  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n307\t  is held.\n308\t\n309\tEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\n310\tone, the game is not affected.\n311\t\n312\t---\n313\t\n314\t## Test it\n315\t\n316\tThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\n317\tand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\n318\tto the game:\n319\t\n320\t```bash\n321\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n322\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\n323\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\n324\tpython3 -m http.server 8000\n325\t```\n326\t\n327\tThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\n328\tThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n329\t`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n330\t`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n331\t`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n332\t\n333\tThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n334\t`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n335\t`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n336\t\n337\t- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n338\t- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n339\t- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n340\t  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n341\t- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n342\t  player of its own;\n343\t- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n344\t  told the round ended;\n345\t- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n346\t\n347\t**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\n348\tcases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n349\t`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\n350\tgame is served from the harness's own origin.\n351\t\n352\tIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\n353\tchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\n354\truns there.\n355\t\n356\t---\n357\t\n358\t## Reference\n359\t\n360\t### Tags\n361\t\n362\t| Tag | |\n363\t|---|---|\n364\t| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n365\t| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n366\t| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n367\t| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n368\t| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n369\t| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n370\t| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n371\t\n372\t### API\n373\t\n374\t| | |\n375\t|---|---|\n376\t| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n377\t| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n378\t| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n379\t| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n380\t| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n381\t| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n382\t| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n383\t| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n384\t| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n385\t| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n386\t| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n387\t| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n388\t| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n389\t\n390\t`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\n391\tread them.\n392\t\n393\t| Event | Arguments |\n394\t|---|---|\n395\t| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n396\t| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n397\t| `message` | `data, from` |\n398\t| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n399\t| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n400\t| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n401\t\n402\tEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\n403\torder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\n404\treach the game through the client unchanged.\n405\t\n406\t### Versions\n407\t\n408\tThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\n409\tgames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\n410\tprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n411\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"c7994dea-48c5-4da6-bf9a-147b7bd5a4fb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:34.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/README.md","content":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","numLines":411,"startLine":1,"totalLines":411}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GqW3K8QyxpAz2rUqzmyFSf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94RQTM1JF4RLN2WZZ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012DXYMp65gnvj4fXaHpkMYa","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/retrofit.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1389,"cache_read_input_tokens":31410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1389},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"c094915a-98cc-4f62-b168-0484c72083d3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:35.245Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94R9px9QSWHiu5Ucd1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012DXYMp65gnvj4fXaHpkMYa":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/retrofit.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012DXYMp65gnvj4fXaHpkMYa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n2\t\n3\tThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\n4\tstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\n5\tpage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\n6\tis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n7\t[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\n8\tfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\n9\tand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\tYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\n14\tin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\n15\ttime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\n16\thow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\n17\taudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n18\t\n19\tA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n20\t`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\n21\tthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\n22\tagainst it:\n23\t\n24\t<protocol>\n25\t{{contract}}\n26\t</protocol>\n27\t\n28\tDo only this:\n29\t\n30\t1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n31\t   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n32\t2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n33\t   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n34\t   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n35\t   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n36\t   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n37\t   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n38\t3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n39\t   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n40\t   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n41\t   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n42\t   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n43\t4. Whenever the score changes, call\n44\t   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n45\t   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n46\t5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n47\t   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n48\t   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n49\t   still going.\n50\t6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n51\t7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n52\t   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n53\t   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n54\t   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n55\t8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n56\t\n57\tDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\n58\ttouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\n59\tthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\n60\tthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\n61\tlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\n62\tgame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\n63\teither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\n64\tdescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n65\t\n66\tIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\n67\tline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n68\t\n69\tOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\n70\tyou changed.\n71\t\n72\t{{notes}}\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a882869-9e92-4155-a3b0-121d8fdc660c","uuid":"056508f1-27b5-48ce-8a31-436a799aff91","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:35.263Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211959__x9a5b1d/work/_research/hub/retrofit.md","content":"# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n\nThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\nstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\npage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\nis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\nfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\nand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n\n---\n\nYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\nin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\ntime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\nhow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\naudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n\nA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\nthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\nagainst it:\n\n<protocol>\n{{contract}}\n</protocol>\n\nDo only this:\n\n1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n4. Whenever the score changes, call\n   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n   still going.\n6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n\nDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\ntouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\nthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\nthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\nlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\ngame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\neither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\ndescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n\nIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\nline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n\nOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\nyou changed.\n\n{{notes}}\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012DXYMp65gnvj4fXaHpkMYa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
